    {"source_id":"D166-CMU-PURE-PURSUIT-1992","domain_id":"D166","publisher":"Carnegie Mellon University, Robotics Institute","title":"Implementation of the Pure Pursuit Path Tracking Algorithm (CMU-RI-TR-92-01)","url":"https://publications.ri.cmu.edu/storage/publications/pub_files/pub3/coulter_r_craig_1992_1/coulter_r_craig_1992_1.pdf","source_family":"primary university technical report / historical autonomous-robotics methods source","locator":"Full 15-page report reopened 2026-10-26; sections 1.2 Description, 2 Theoretical Derivation, 3.3 Pursuit Algorithm, 4 Properties, 4.3 Comments. Defines pure pursuit as geometric path tracking: choose a lookahead goal point, calculate joining-arc curvature, convert curvature to steering; details lookahead effects and notes unmodeled vehicle/actuator dynamics and steering lag.","supports":"A primary historical explanation of the pure-pursuit lateral path-tracking technique, its geometric calculation, path representation and lookahead parameter, including explicit limitations that motivate vehicle-dynamics and actuator modeling in more advanced control designs.","claim_limit":"One 1992 technical report describing CMU mobile-robot/NavLab implementation, not a comparative modern AV benchmark, proof of current prevalence or a controller performance guarantee. Its reported field experience and implementation assumptions are historically and platform bounded.","search_status":"full primary technical report opened and inspected 2026-10-26","credibility_assessment":"Primary method implementation report from the Carnegie Mellon Robotics Institute, with full text and derivation accessible. Historical author-reported experience is not independent comparative evidence or a universal vehicle-safety result."},
