    {"source_id":"D011-CFA-BASICS-PORTFOLIO-PLANNING-CONSTRUCTION-2026","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"CFA Institute","title":"Basics of Portfolio Planning and Construction","url":"https://www.cfainstitute.org/insights/professional-learning/refresher-readings/2026/basics-of-portfolio-planning-and-construction","source_family":"official professional-body curriculum reading","locator":"2026 Level I reading overview and learning outcomes: investment policy statement (IPS), objectives and constraints, liquidity/time horizon/tax/legal constraints, strategic asset allocation, benchmark-relative versus absolute objectives and rebalancing policy.","supports":"An inspectable foundation for why portfolio decisions start from mandate, objectives, constraints and asset allocation rather than selecting an optimizer or signal first.","claim_limit":"CFA curriculum evidence describes its own portfolio-planning framework; it does not establish a universal sequence for all systematic trading, mandates, asset classes or roles.","search_status":"Official CFA Institute 2026 reading page opened 2026-10-26","credibility_assessment":"Primary source for the CFA reading's own learning outcomes and prescribed planning concepts; not independent evidence of universal practice."},
    {"source_id":"D011-OUP-DEMIGUEL-NAIVE-DIVERSIFICATION-2009","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"Oxford University Press / The Review of Financial Studies","title":"Optimal Versus Naive Diversification: How Inefficient is the 1/N Portfolio Strategy?","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/rfs/hhm075","source_family":"peer-reviewed primary portfolio research","locator":"Abstract, Review of Financial Studies 22(5), 2009, pp. 1915–1953; compares 14 sample-based mean-variance and estimation-error-reducing strategies against equal-weight 1/N across seven empirical datasets using out-of-sample Sharpe ratio, certainty-equivalent return and turnover.","supports":"The equal-weight 1/N portfolio is a useful estimation-light benchmark/alternative to optimized allocation; finite-sample estimation error can offset theoretical optimization gains in the tested datasets.","claim_limit":"The result is bounded to the paper's models, datasets, rebalancing and evaluation criteria; it does not show 1/N is always superior or that optimized allocation is never useful.","search_status":"Publisher abstract inspected 2026-10-26","credibility_assessment":"Peer-reviewed primary comparative study; publisher abstract directly reports the study scope and result, while detailed method inspection should use the full paper."},
    {"source_id":"D011-CFA-BLACK-LITTERMAN-GLOBAL-PORTFOLIO-OPTIMIZATION-1992","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"CFA Institute / Financial Analysts Journal","title":"Global Portfolio Optimization","url":"https://rpc.cfainstitute.org/research/financial-analysts-journal/1992/faj-v48-n5-28","source_family":"peer-reviewed primary portfolio research; publisher abstract","locator":"Black and Litterman, Financial Analysts Journal 48(5), 1992, pp. 28–43; publisher abstract describes CAPM equilibrium returns as neutral starting points, then incorporates investor views with confidence levels in constrained global asset allocation.","supports":"Black–Litterman is a view-informed extension/alternative around mean-variance allocation that uses equilibrium returns as a prior starting point and expresses investor views rather than treating raw sample means as the only return input.","claim_limit":"Publisher abstract establishes the conceptual design only; it does not prove out-of-sample superiority, current adoption prevalence, or suitability for every portfolio mandate.","search_status":"CFA Institute publisher record and abstract inspected 2026-10-26","credibility_assessment":"Primary publisher record for the original peer-reviewed article; abstract-level evidence only, with full-text access restricted."},
    {"source_id":"D011-JPM-MAILLARD-RONCALLI-TEILETCHE-RISK-CONTRIBUTIONS-2010","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"The Journal of Portfolio Management / authors' research manuscript","title":"On the Properties of Equally-Weighted Risk Contributions Portfolios","url":"https://doi.org/10.3905/jpm.2010.36.4.060","source_family":"peer-reviewed primary portfolio research; author-hosted manuscript","locator":"Maillard, Roncalli and Teïletche, Journal of Portfolio Management 36(4), 2010; author-hosted manuscript inspected. Defines equal risk contribution, derives portfolio properties and compares theoretical/empirical risk levels with minimum-variance and equal-weight portfolios.","supports":"Risk parity/equal-risk-contribution allocates capital to equalize component risk contributions, changing the portfolio objective from equal capital weights or minimum variance; its output is a risk-balanced set of portfolio weights.","claim_limit":"The paper supports mathematical properties and its empirical setup, not universal diversification quality or out-of-sample superiority. Results depend on the risk measure, covariance inputs, long-only/constraint assumptions and investment universe.","search_status":"Author manuscript and publisher DOI record inspected 2026-10-26","credibility_assessment":"Peer-reviewed primary article with author-hosted manuscript inspected; methodological source for its defined risk-contribution portfolio, not independent proof of universal practitioner use."},
    {"source_id":"D011-SPRINGER-TUTUNCU-KOENIG-ROBUST-ASSET-ALLOCATION-2004","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"Springer / Annals of Operations Research","title":"Robust Asset Allocation","url":"https://doi.org/10.1023/B:ANOR.0000045281.41041.ed","source_family":"peer-reviewed primary operations-research article","locator":"Tütüncü and Koenig, Annals of Operations Research 132, 2004, pp. 157–187; publisher abstract describes uncertainty sets for return moments and maximin asset allocation when estimated return structure is unreliable.","supports":"Robust allocation explicitly models uncertainty around estimated return parameters and seeks an allocation with favorable worst-case behavior; this is distinct from a point-estimate MVO and from Bayesian posterior/view updating.","claim_limit":"Publisher abstract establishes the formulation and reported study scope, not guaranteed robustness, universal out-of-sample gains or the best uncertainty-set choice. Robustness depends on calibrated uncertainty sets and investor constraints.","search_status":"Publisher abstract inspected 2026-10-26","credibility_assessment":"Peer-reviewed primary article record from the publisher; abstract-level description only, with no independent performance claim inferred."},
    {"source_id":"D011-CFA-LIABILITY-DRIVEN-INDEX-STRATEGIES-2026","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"CFA Institute","title":"Liability-Driven and Index-Based Strategies","url":"https://www.cfainstitute.org/insights/professional-learning/refresher-readings/2026/liability-driven-index-based-strategies","source_family":"official professional-body curriculum reading","locator":"2026 Level III reading overview and learning outcomes: asset-liability management, liability-relative fixed-income construction, single/multiple liabilities, cash-flow and duration matching/immunization, hedging ratios, interest-rate scenarios and bond-index alternatives.","supports":"Liability-aware allocation is a conditional portfolio branch when future obligations are part of the mandate: characterize liability cash-flow timing/certainty, then choose matching/hedging and growth assets; output includes liability-relative exposures, hedge ratios and funding-risk outcomes.","claim_limit":"CFA curriculum describes its fixed-income/pension/insurance examples; this does not make liability-driven investing compulsory for portfolios without material liabilities or establish one universal hedge design.","search_status":"Official CFA Institute 2026 reading opened 2026-10-26","credibility_assessment":"Primary source for this curriculum's learning outcomes and framework; not a universal mandate or independent empirical ranking."},
    {"source_id":"D011-ERIM-LIABILITY-RELATIVE-DRAWDOWN-ALM-2010","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"Erasmus Research Institute of Management / Journal of Asset Management","title":"A Liability-Relative Drawdown Approach to Pension Asset Liability Management","url":"https://doi.org/10.1057/jam.2010.13","source_family":"peer-reviewed primary asset-liability management research; university repository record","locator":"Berkelaar and Kouwenberg, Journal of Asset Management 11(2–3), 2010, pp. 194–217; ERIM repository record and abstract describe liability-relative drawdown optimization, simulated asset/liability returns and pension asset-liability design.","supports":"A liability-aware alternative can optimize surplus/liability-relative downside risk rather than asset-only variance; the relevant output is portfolio exposure and drawdown/funding risk relative to obligations.","claim_limit":"The abstract reports results for a particular pension ALM model and simulation setup; it does not establish universal superiority or applicability to asset-only mandates.","search_status":"University repository abstract inspected 2026-10-26","credibility_assessment":"Peer-reviewed article indexed in the authors' university research repository; abstract-level claim evidence, bounded to the model and pension context."},
    {"source_id":"D011-CAMBRIDGE-FROST-SAVARINO-EMPIRICAL-BAYES-PORTFOLIO-1986","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"Cambridge University Press / Journal of Financial and Quantitative Analysis","title":"An Empirical Bayes Approach to Efficient Portfolio Selection","url":"https://doi.org/10.2307/2331043","source_family":"peer-reviewed primary portfolio research","locator":"Frost and Savarino, Journal of Financial and Quantitative Analysis 21(3), 1986, pp. 293–305; Cambridge Core paper record/abstract and PDF inspected. Abstract frames portfolio choice using a predictive distribution and an informative prior to address estimation risk in historical return characteristics.","supports":"Bayesian portfolio choice incorporates parameter uncertainty through prior/predictive distributions; an empirical-Bayes prior is a distinct way to regularize expected-return/covariance estimates, conceptually related to but not identical with Black–Litterman investor-view inputs.","claim_limit":"The paper's prior assumptions and empirical setup are specific. Bayesian formulation does not remove model risk or guarantee superior results; detailed comparisons require the full method and matched out-of-sample tests.","search_status":"Cambridge publisher paper record and PDF inspected 2026-10-26","credibility_assessment":"Peer-reviewed primary paper hosted by its publisher; source for its own empirical-Bayes formulation, not a universal performance result."},
    {"source_id":"D011-ELSEVIER-ROBUST-BAYESIAN-PORTFOLIO-2025","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"Elsevier / International Review of Financial Analysis","title":"Robust Bayesian Portfolio Optimization","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1057521925003023","source_family":"peer-reviewed primary portfolio research; publisher abstract","locator":"Zapata Quimbayo, Carmona Espejo and Gamboa Hidalgo, International Review of Financial Analysis 103, July 2025, article 104215; abstract describes normal-inverse-Wishart/Gamma prior modeling and ellipsoidal uncertainty sets for Dow Jones Industrial Index stocks.","supports":"A robust-Bayesian design combines probabilistic parameter modeling with worst-case/uncertainty-set protection; it is a distinct research approach, and its reported comparison is against specified Markowitz/robust baselines in one US equity universe.","claim_limit":"Publisher abstract only; one index/universe and paper-specific priors, uncertainty sets and evaluation. The reported performance finding must not be generalized to other markets or treated as established superiority.","search_status":"Publisher abstract inspected 2026-10-26","credibility_assessment":"Peer-reviewed primary article record; abstract-level study evidence, with conclusions bounded to the authors' design and tested universe."},
    {"source_id":"D011-ELSEVIER-FAMA-FRENCH-COMMON-RISK-FACTORS-1993","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"Elsevier / Journal of Financial Economics","title":"Common Risk Factors in the Returns on Stocks and Bonds","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/0304-405X(93)90023-5","source_family":"peer-reviewed primary asset-pricing research","locator":"Fama and French, Journal of Financial Economics 33(1), 1993, pp. 3–56; publisher abstract identifies stock-market, size, book-to-market, bond-maturity and default-related factors and states their empirical U.S. stock/bond scope.","supports":"Foundational factor-model context: return variation and cross-sectional expected-return hypotheses can be represented using named systematic factors; estimating exposures or testing a factor is an empirical method, not proof that the factor is causal, persistent or a profitable strategy.","claim_limit":"Historical U.S. stocks/bonds and the paper's factor definitions/sample; not an exhaustive modern factor library, current expected-return guarantee, or evidence those factors generalize to every asset class/market.","search_status":"Publisher abstract and public manuscript inspected 2026-10-26","credibility_assessment":"Peer-reviewed primary empirical article; abstract directly supports its named factors and tested asset scope, while investment-performance extrapolations require separate validation."},
    {"source_id":"D011-SEC-INVESTOR-ORDER-TYPES-UPDATED-2026","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"U.S. Securities and Exchange Commission, Investor.gov","title":"Understanding Order Types – Investor Bulletin","url":"https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/general-resources/news-alerts/alerts-bulletins/investor-bulletins-14","source_family":"official regulator investor education; current update","locator":"SEC bulletin updated 2026-08-18; sections MARKET, LIMIT and STOP ORDERS explain execution-price versus execution-probability trade-offs and warn that broker order availability/semantics vary.","supports":"Clear baseline distinction for the order-instruction branch: market orders prioritize immediate execution but price is not guaranteed; limit orders cap/floor acceptable price but may remain unfilled. These instructions change fill, price and opportunity-risk considerations for execution design.","claim_limit":"Retail-facing U.S. stock-order descriptions, not a complete professional order taxonomy, venue rulebook, derivatives/FX order guide or algorithmic-execution method. Product and broker semantics vary, as the SEC bulletin notes.","search_status":"Official SEC bulletin opened; update date verified 2026-10-26","credibility_assessment":"Primary regulator education source for its stated general descriptions; limited to investor-level U.S. stock examples and explicitly non-exhaustive."},
    {"source_id":"D011-LEAN-RESEARCH-GUIDE-LOOKAHEAD-SURVIVORSHIP-2026","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"QuantConnect / LEAN documentation","title":"Research Guide: avoid look-ahead and survivorship bias","url":"https://www.quantconnect.com/docs/v2/writing-algorithms/key-concepts/research-guide","source_family":"maintained implementation-platform documentation","locator":"Official documentation sections ‘Look-Ahead Bias’ and ‘Survivorship Bias’; discusses dynamic universes, point-in-time data, reporting lags, delisted securities and how current index constituents can leak future membership into historical tests.","supports":"A concrete data-validity prerequisite for systematic backtests: universe membership and feature availability must reflect information available at each historical decision time; current constituents and future-adjusted features can create look-ahead/survivorship bias.","claim_limit":"Vendor documentation is credible for its platform concepts/examples, but its dataset-vetting and platform-specific assurances are not independent guarantees for other data vendors or all asset classes.","search_status":"Maintained official docs opened 2026-10-26","credibility_assessment":"First-party platform documentation with explicit explanations and examples; use for implementation guidance, not independent validation of vendor datasets."},
    {"source_id":"D011-LEAN-DATA-NORMALIZATION-CORPORATE-ACTIONS-2026","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"QuantConnect / LEAN documentation","title":"Dataset Misconceptions: normalization and corporate-action history","url":"https://www.quantconnect.com/docs/v2/cloud-platform/datasets/misconceptions","source_family":"maintained implementation-platform documentation","locator":"Data Normalization section: adjusted equity histories use the full split/dividend history; results can differ when a later corporate action changes historical adjusted prices, making data normalization mode and as-of semantics material to reproducibility.","supports":"Historical price adjustment, splits, dividends and future corporate-action knowledge can alter backtest inputs; researchers need to document the normalization mode and ensure features/portfolio accounting use an appropriate time-aware convention.","claim_limit":"Describes QuantConnect/LEAN data behavior; do not generalize its exact adjustment rules or issue to every vendor, instrument, market or data product.","search_status":"Maintained official docs opened 2026-10-26","credibility_assessment":"Primary source for this platform's normalization behavior and implementation caveats; not a cross-vendor market-data standard."},
    {"source_id":"D011-LEAN-HISTORICAL-UNIVERSE-DATA-2026","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"QuantConnect / LEAN documentation","title":"Historical Universe Data","url":"https://www.quantconnect.com/docs/v2/writing-algorithms/historical-data/universe-data","source_family":"maintained implementation-platform documentation","locator":"Official docs state that universe history returns timestamped universe observations; dynamic universe selection is intended to reduce selection bias and historical constituent data must be used at the relevant time. The history method itself returns dataset records and does not apply the user filter function automatically.","supports":"Implementation evidence for point-in-time universe reconstruction and a specific caveat: retrieving historical universe data is distinct from applying a strategy's universe filter, so the researcher must implement and validate selection logic.","claim_limit":"Describes LEAN APIs and data access. It does not prove unbiased results automatically or establish coverage/quality for all vendors, asset classes or jurisdictions.","search_status":"Maintained official docs opened 2026-10-26","credibility_assessment":"First-party API documentation for the platform's historical-universe semantics; implementation evidence rather than independent methodological validation."},
    {"source_id":"D011-CFA-CURRICULUM-2026","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"CFA Institute","title":"CFA Program Curriculum Overview","url":"https://www.cfainstitute.org/programs/cfa-program/curriculum","source_family":"professional curriculum","locator":"Topic outlines for quantitative methods, derivatives and risk management, portfolio construction; curriculum is updated through practice analysis.","supports":"Domain boundaries among quantitative analysis, instrument valuation/risk and investment decisions.","claim_limit":"Professional curriculum scope; not every engineering or quant-development role and not a route prerequisite by itself.","search_status":"opened_and_inspected_2026-10-23"},
    {"source_id":"D011-CFA-TIMESERIES-2026","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"CFA Institute","title":"Time-Series Analysis (2026 Level II Quantitative Methods)","url":"https://www.cfainstitute.org/insights/professional-learning/refresher-readings/2026/time-series-analysis","source_family":"professional curriculum / methods","locator":"Introduction and learning outcomes: time-series observations used for investment decisions; modeling past and forecasting future values; estimation and changing dynamics.","supports":"Finance-specific use of time-series methods and their analysis/forecasting context.","claim_limit":"Curriculum does not exhaust time-series approaches or prove every role needs this content.","search_status":"opened_and_inspected_2026-10-23"},
