    {"source_id":"D011-R-CORRELATION-TESTS","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"R Core Team","title":"R stats: Pearson, Spearman and Kendall Correlation Tests","url":"https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/cor.test.html","source_family":"maintained statistical software documentation","locator":"R stats manual cor.test; opened 2026-10-23. Documents Pearson product-moment, Spearman rank and Kendall tau tests, their estimands, inference and exact/asymptotic options.","supports":"Official implementation-level comparison of correlation techniques and their alternatives, useful for finance data diagnostics, association analysis and signal screening where the task/source supports it.","claim_limit":"API documentation is not method-theory instruction or proof that any correlation is stable/predictive in financial data. Pearson and rank methods answer related but distinct association questions; sample dependence and time structure require separate treatment.","search_status":"opened_and_inspected_2026-10-23"},
    {"source_id":"D011-R-CHI-SQUARE-TEST","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"R Core Team","title":"R stats: Pearson Chi-Squared Tests for Count Data","url":"https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/chisq.test.html","source_family":"maintained statistical software documentation","locator":"R stats manual chisq.test; opened 2026-10-23. Documents goodness-of-fit and contingency-table tests, expected counts, 2x2 continuity correction and Monte Carlo p-values.","supports":"Canonical procedure and implementation options for categorical/count-data inference that can be used in finance studies when the sampling design and expected-count conditions support the test.","claim_limit":"Chi-square is not a general-purpose numerical test or a substitute for regression; asymptotic validity and table design matter. It does not establish a finance-specific task route by itself.","search_status":"opened_and_inspected_2026-10-23"},
    {"source_id":"D011-R-FISHER-EXACT-TEST","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"R Core Team","title":"R stats: Fisher's Exact Test for Count Data","url":"https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/fisher.test.html","source_family":"maintained statistical software documentation","locator":"R stats manual fisher.test; opened 2026-10-23. Documents conditional exact tests for contingency-table independence, exact/Monte Carlo options and limitations for larger tables.","supports":"A method alternative for suitable sparse contingency tables, enabling comparison with asymptotic Pearson chi-square inference under different count conditions.","claim_limit":"Fisher's exact test is conditional on fixed margins and can become computationally difficult for larger tables; it is not automatically preferable for every chi-square problem or every financial dataset.","search_status":"opened_and_inspected_2026-10-23"},
    {"source_id":"D011-R-LM-ANOVA-ANCOVA","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"R Core Team","title":"R stats: Linear Models, Regression, ANOVA and ANCOVA","url":"https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/lm.html","source_family":"maintained statistical software documentation","locator":"R stats manual lm; opened 2026-10-23. Describes fitting linear models for regression, single-stratum ANOVA and ANCOVA, weighted fits and model contrasts; notes data/time-series alignment considerations.","supports":"Shows how regression and group-mean comparison procedures sit within a linear-model framework, including where weighted regression and time alignment require distinct choices.","claim_limit":"Software reference is not a complete inferential course. Correct use depends on design, dependence, error structure, coding and diagnostics; shared API does not make all procedures or results equivalent.","search_status":"opened_and_inspected_2026-10-23"},
    {"source_id":"D011-R-GLM-BINARY-LOGISTIC","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"R Core Team","title":"R stats: Generalized Linear Models, Including Binomial/Logistic Regression","url":"https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/glm.html","source_family":"maintained statistical software documentation","locator":"R stats manual glm; opened 2026-10-23. Documents specifying response distribution and link, including binomial responses and iterative weighted least squares fitting.","supports":"Implementation reference for linking binary-outcome logistic regression to the broader generalized-linear-model family; clarifies that outcome scale and chosen link distinguish it from ordinary least-squares modeling.","claim_limit":"Does not teach full assumptions, separation, calibration, causal interpretation or market suitability. A binary outcome does not by itself prove logistic regression is the only suitable classifier.","search_status":"opened_and_inspected_2026-10-23"},
    {"source_id":"D011-STATSMODELS-REGRESSION-FAMILY","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"statsmodels project","title":"statsmodels: Linear Regression Model Family","url":"https://www.statsmodels.org/stable/regression.html","source_family":"maintained statistical software documentation","locator":"Official guide; opened 2026-10-23. Describes OLS, WLS, GLS, GLSAR, rolling OLS/WLS and quantile regression with error-structure and model documentation.","supports":"Technical comparison among ordinary, weighted, generalized, autoregressive-error, rolling and quantile regression implementations used for differing response/error and time-context needs.","claim_limit":"Available APIs are not a recommendation hierarchy, finance-specific validation or evidence of universal industry use. Model assumptions and financial task relevance need independent sources.","search_status":"opened_and_inspected_2026-10-23"},
    {"source_id":"D011-STATSMODELS-DISCRETE-RESPONSE","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"statsmodels project","title":"statsmodels: Regression with Discrete Dependent Variables","url":"https://www.statsmodels.org/stable/discretemod.html","source_family":"maintained statistical software documentation","locator":"Official guide; opened 2026-10-23. Lists Logit/Probit for binary responses, multinomial logit for nominal outcomes, and Poisson/negative-binomial models for count outcomes.","supports":"Outcome-type-based alternatives among established econometric models and their implementation relationship to binary, nominal and count-data tasks.","claim_limit":"API availability does not prove that these models are interchangeable or appropriate for any target. Causal claims, rare events, overdispersion and prediction objectives need task-specific review.","search_status":"opened_and_inspected_2026-10-23"},
    {"source_id":"D011-SCIKIT-MODEL-EVALUATION","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"scikit-learn project","title":"scikit-learn: Model Selection and Evaluation","url":"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/model_selection.html","source_family":"maintained machine-learning software documentation","locator":"Official user guide; opened 2026-10-23. Covers cross-validation iterators, model/parameter selection, validation, and classification/regression scoring metrics.","supports":"Practical comparison/evaluation layer for traditional estimators and ML models; supports the general sequence from fitting to out-of-sample model selection and evaluation.","claim_limit":"General-purpose documentation does not establish leakage-safe evaluation for financial time series. Random folds can violate temporal information order; use time-aware splits and finance-specific validation evidence.","search_status":"opened_and_inspected_2026-10-23"},
    {"source_id":"D011-SCIKIT-TIME-SERIES-SPLIT","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"scikit-learn project","title":"scikit-learn: Cross-Validation for Time-Series Data","url":"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/cross_validation.html","source_family":"maintained machine-learning software documentation","locator":"Official cross-validation guide; opened 2026-10-23. Explains why ordinary KFold/ShuffleSplit i.i.d. assumptions can be inappropriate under temporal dependence and describes TimeSeriesSplit evaluating future observations; equal-interval and splitter limitations are stated.","supports":"Primary implementation guidance for preserving time order when comparing forecasting/ML models; identifies a concrete methodological difference from ordinary shuffled/random cross-validation.","claim_limit":"Generic TimeSeriesSplit is not sufficient for every financial label, overlapping horizon, panel or strategy backtest; PBO, purge/embargo and leakage controls need task-specific evaluation. Does not validate any return forecast.","search_status":"opened_and_inspected_2026-10-23"},
    {"source_id":"D011-PBO-BACKTEST-OVERFIT-2017","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"Journal of Computational Finance / UC eScholarship","title":"The Probability of Backtest Overfitting","url":"https://escholarship.org/uc/item/4w1110bb","source_family":"peer-reviewed quantitative-finance methodology research","locator":"Bailey, Borwein, LÃƒÂ³pez de Prado and Zhu (2017), Journal of Computational Finance 20(4), DOI 10.21314/jcf.2016.322; opened article record and paper. Defines PBO and combinatorially symmetric cross-validation for investment-strategy selection risk.","supports":"A finance-specific complement to ordinary predictive model evaluation: assessing whether the in-sample strategy configuration selected from many trials tends to underperform out-of-sample.","claim_limit":"Proposed framework with its own assumptions and use conditions; it does not replace forward holdout, economic transaction-cost analysis, or all other validation methods and is not a regulator requirement.","search_status":"published_article_record_and_paper_opened_2026-10-23"},
    {"source_id":"D011-RFS-ML-ASSET-PRICING-2020","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"The Review of Financial Studies / Oxford University Press","title":"Empirical Asset Pricing via Machine Learning","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/rfs/hhaa009","source_family":"peer-reviewed finance research","locator":"Gu, Kelly and Xiu (2020), RFS 33(5), 2223-2273; opened 2026-10-23. Comparative methodology, U.S. equities study, out-of-sample design and discussion of regression benchmarks, regularization, trees and neural networks.","supports":"Finance-specific evidence that modern ML methods can extend linear/regression baselines by handling richer nonlinear predictor interactions and large predictor sets; the paper also discusses overfit risk and out-of-sample evaluation.","claim_limit":"One empirical asset-pricing study on selected U.S. equity data, predictors and periods. It does not prove ML universally outperforms traditional methods, transfer to all markets/roles, or prescribe a dependency graph.","search_status":"peer_reviewed_article_opened_and_inspected_2026-10-23"},
    {"source_id":"D011-WILEY-ASSET-PRICING-ML-REVIEW-2024","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"Journal of Economic Surveys / Wiley","title":"Asset Pricing and Machine Learning: A Critical Review","url":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1111/joes.12532","source_family":"peer-reviewed review article","locator":"Bagnara et al.; online 2022, Journal of Economic Surveys 38(1), 27-56 (2024 issue); open access. Groups asset-pricing ML research into regularization, dimension reduction, trees/random forests, neural networks and comparative analyses.","supports":"A source-backed taxonomy of how ML techniques relate to econometric asset-pricing practice and critical considerations when comparing flexibility, prediction and economic interpretation.","claim_limit":"Review scope is asset pricing and literature available through its review period; it does not cover all quantitative finance or establish universal recommendations.","search_status":"open_access_review_opened_and_inspected_2026-10-23"},
    {"source_id":"D011-ARCH-VOLATILITY-PROCESSES-2026","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"arch Python package maintainers","title":"arch 7.2: Volatility Processes and Alternatives","url":"https://arch.readthedocs.io/en/stable/univariate/volatility.html","source_family":"maintained statistical software documentation","locator":"Stable arch 7.2 documentation lists constant variance, ARCH, GARCH family, EGARCH, FIGARCH, HARCH, MIDAS-HARCH, APARCH, EWMA and RiskMetrics2006 processes; explains a volatility process is combined with a mean model.","supports":"Primary implementation index for conditional-volatility families and alternatives; makes clear mean dynamics and variance dynamics are separate, composable model components.","claim_limit":"API availability is not proof a model is appropriate for a specific asset, forecast horizon or regulatory risk task. This software page is not a substitute for model assumptions, theoretical methods or empirical comparison.","search_status":"official arch documentation for stable 7.2 opened 2026-10-23"},
    {"source_id":"D011-ARCH-UNIVARIATE-MODELS-2026","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"arch Python package maintainers","title":"arch 7.2: Univariate Volatility Model Specification and Forecasts","url":"https://arch.readthedocs.io/en/stable/univariate/univariate.html","source_family":"maintained statistical software documentation","locator":"Stable docs show high- and low-level specification, mean and volatility component choices, and analytic or simulation/residual-bootstrap forecasts where tractable; linked modeling guide uses financial returns and reports conditional volatility/forecast outputs.","supports":"Implementation context for combining mean and volatility specifications, producing volatility forecasts, and comparing alternatives consistently.","claim_limit":"Software examples and supported forecasts do not determine a correct model, forecast loss, assumptions or finance task fit; tutorial data are illustrative and forecast quality must be assessed out of sample.","search_status":"official arch documentation for stable 7.2 opened 2026-10-23"},
    {"source_id":"D011-FED-REALIZED-VOL-ML-COMPARE-2025-REV2026","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System, Finance and Economics Discussion Series","title":"Linear and Nonlinear Econometric Models versus Machine-Learning Models: Realized-Volatility Forecasting","url":"https://www.federalreserve.gov/econres/feds/linear-and-nonlinear-econometric-models-against-machine-learning-models.htm","source_family":"official central-bank working paper / credible grey literature","locator":"Rehim Kilic, FEDS 2025-061r1, August 2025, revised September 2026. Compares HAR, ARFIMA, threshold/smooth-transition/Markov-switching HAR with XGBoost and neural networks on S&P 500/40 U.S. equities; evaluates multiple horizons using MSFE, MAE, QLIKE, model-confidence sets, DieboldÃ¢â‚¬â€œMariano tests, realized utility and VaR/ES. Results vary by horizon; ML sometimes improves HAR but does not systematically beat the broader econometric set.","supports":"Recent finance-specific comparison of traditional/extended econometric and machine-learning approaches to realized-volatility forecasting, including how long-memory, nonlinear and regime-dependent methods compare and why target horizon/evaluation criterion matter.","claim_limit":"Working paper, not Federal Reserve policy or a final consensus; U.S. equity samples and chosen predictor sets/horizons constrain transfer. The authors' stated disclaimer says findings are their own; recheck revision before relying on the result.","credibility_assessment":"High-value grey research source because it is a dated, versioned Federal Reserve working paper with a full methods/results paper, explicit markets, forecast protocols and multiple robustness/evaluation checks. It is preliminary author research rather than Board policy or peer-reviewed consensus; independently compare with peer-reviewed volatility research and other markets.","search_status":"official FEDS page and September 2026 revision metadata/abstract opened 2026-10-23; paper indexed, full analysis not independently replicated"},
    {"source_id":"D011-JEF-REALIZED-VOL-ML-2024","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"Journal of Empirical Finance / Elsevier","title":"Forecasting Realized Volatility: Does Anything Beat Linear Models?","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jempfin.2024.101524","source_family":"peer-reviewed finance research","locator":"Branco, Rubesam and Zevallos, Journal of Empirical Finance, September 2024. Compares linear and nonlinear ML models against HAR for ten global equity indices (2000Ã¢â‚¬â€œ2021), at daily/weekly/monthly horizons; abstract reports no systematic ML outperformance, no single model dominates statistical/economic measures, and the preferred model varies with horizon/objective.","supports":"Independent peer-reviewed cross-market check against the Federal Reserve working paper; supports teaching HAR/linear baselines, ML as a tested alternative, and horizon/objective-specific comparison rather than automatic replacement.","claim_limit":"Ten indices and a bounded historical sample; abstract-level findings do not establish a universal ranking and detailed study design should be inspected before fine-grained route/technique claims.","search_status":"publisher article record and abstract inspected 2026-10-23; full text access restricted"},
    {"source_id":"D011-CHICAGO-FED-STOCHASTIC-VOLATILITY-2009","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"Federal Reserve Bank of Chicago","title":"Stochastic Volatility","url":"https://www.chicagofed.org/publications/working-papers/2009/wp-04","source_family":"central-bank research review / credible grey literature","locator":"Andersen and Benzoni, Chicago Fed Working Paper 2009-04. Distinguishes genuine stochastic-volatility models, where latent volatility has its own shocks, from observation-driven ARCH/GARCH specifications; discusses estimation/inference challenges and applications in derivatives, rates, FX and realized-volatility analysis.","supports":"Conceptual comparison of GARCH and latent stochastic-volatility model families and why model choice changes inference, computational approach and downstream pricing/risk tasks.","claim_limit":"A 2009 working-paper review, not a current implementation manual or evidence that one family is universally superior; use as conceptual framework alongside recent technical and empirical sources.","credibility_assessment":"Credible grey research from an identifiable Federal Reserve Bank working-paper series with named academic authors, date and traceable source citations. It is older and is author research, not central-bank policy; corroborate the conceptual distinctions and current estimation approaches with newer methods sources.","search_status":"official Chicago Fed working-paper page opened 2026-10-23"},
    {"source_id":"D011-JFUTURES-GARCH-IMPLIED-REALIZED-2016","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"Journal of Futures Markets / Wiley; manuscript hosted by University of Stirling repository","title":"Forecasting Stock Return Volatility: GARCH, Implied Volatility and Realized Volatility Models","url":"https://www.storre.stir.ac.uk/handle/1893/23189","source_family":"peer-reviewed financial econometrics / institutional repository grey access copy","locator":"Kambouroudis, McMillan and Tsakou (2016), Journal of Futures Markets 36(12), 1127Ã¢â‚¬â€œ1163. Repository marks the article refereed and provides the accepted manuscript. Compares GARCH, option-implied, realized-volatility and combined ARMA/GARCH forecasts; combination results depend on the study and include VaR backtesting.","supports":"Shows that historical-return conditional variance, option-implied forward-looking measures and high-frequency realized measures are distinct information sources that can be compared or combined for risk forecasts; they are not simply interchangeable software alternatives.","claim_limit":"2016 stock-index study; results may differ by market, horizon, option liquidity, volatility proxy and loss/VaR criterion. Repository metadata and accepted manuscript should be checked against the final publisher version for citation precision.","credibility_assessment":"Credible grey access record for a peer-reviewed article: the university repository identifies the refereed journal/version and DOI and provides an accepted manuscript, while the journal is the version of record. The institutional host improves access but does not substitute for the publisher's final copy or broader replication.","search_status":"institutional repository record, referee status, DOI and abstract inspected 2026-10-23"},
    {"source_id":"D011-CORSI-HAR-RV-2009","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"Journal of Financial Econometrics / Oxford University Press","title":"A Simple Approximate Long-Memory Model of Realized Volatility","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/jjfinec/nbp001","source_family":"peer-reviewed financial econometrics research","locator":"Fulvio Corsi (2009), Journal of Financial Econometrics 7(2), 174Ã¢â‚¬â€œ196. Proposes HAR-RV as a parsimonious heterogeneous daily/weekly/monthly realized-volatility cascade; demonstrates forecasting properties and long-memory-like empirical features without claiming a true long-memory process.","supports":"Origin/interpretation of HAR-RV and its relationship to horizon aggregation in realized-volatility modeling; a transparent econometric baseline for comparison with richer econometric and ML approaches.","claim_limit":"The model's approximation and reported sample results do not imply literal long memory or universal forecast superiority; inspect sampling/estimation details and compare under the learner's task and horizon.","search_status":"publisher DOI record and abstract cross-checked through academic index 2026-10-23"},
    {"source_id":"D011-NBER-REALIZED-VOL-2001","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"National Bureau of Economic Research","title":"Modeling and Forecasting Realized Volatility","url":"https://www.nber.org/papers/w8160","source_family":"academic working paper / credible grey literature","locator":"Andersen, Bollerslev, Diebold and Labys, NBER Working Paper 8160 (2001), subsequently published in Econometrica 71(2), 579Ã¢â‚¬â€œ625 (2003). Links high-frequency realized variance to daily/lower-frequency forecasts; compares long-memory Gaussian VAR on realized volatility with daily ARCH models in FX data and discusses density/quantile forecasts.","supports":"Foundational bridge from high-frequency measurement to conventional time-series modeling and risk-distribution outputs; demonstrates that realized-volatility series can be modeled using AR/VAR-type methods rather than only ARCH/GARCH on returns.","claim_limit":"Older FX sample and period; the NBER working-paper record is a discovery/access source for a later journal article. Verify final published version for detailed technical claims and assess modern microstructure/noise corrections separately.","credibility_assessment":"Strong academic working-paper record with named authors, date, full citation, later peer-reviewed publication and a specific empirical design; the 2001 results are historical and market-bounded, so do not treat them as a current performance ranking.","search_status":"NBER record and published-version citation inspected 2026-10-23"},
    {"source_id":"D011-CFA-CONTINGENT-CLAIMS-MODELS-2026","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"CFA Institute","title":"Valuation of Contingent Claims: Binomial, BlackÃ¢â‚¬â€œScholesÃ¢â‚¬â€œMerton and Black Models (2026)","url":"https://www.cfainstitute.org/insights/professional-learning/refresher-readings/2026/valuation-contingent-claims","source_family":"professional curriculum / derivative-pricing methods","locator":"2026 Level II reading distinguishes discrete-time binomial and continuous-time BlackÃ¢â‚¬â€œScholesÃ¢â‚¬â€œMerton approaches; applies Black model to futures and interest-rate options/swaptions; includes no-arbitrage, European/American options, Greeks, delta hedging and implied volatility outcomes.","supports":"Finance-specific foundations, purposes and instrument contexts for replication/no-arbitrage option pricing families; helps connect payoff/instrument class to analytic or tree methods and hedge outputs.","claim_limit":"Professional curriculum reading, not a complete numerical-method catalogue or proof every quant role needs every model. Full chapter access may require login; curricula do not prove universal prerequisite order.","search_status":"official 2026 CFA reading page and outcomes inspected 2026-10-23"},
    {"source_id":"D011-CFA-ARBITRAGE-FREE-FIXED-INCOME-2026","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"CFA Institute","title":"The Arbitrage-Free Valuation Framework (2026 Fixed Income)","url":"https://www.cfainstitute.org/insights/professional-learning/refresher-readings/2026/arbitrage-free-valuation-framework","source_family":"professional curriculum / fixed-income pricing methods","locator":"2026 Level II reading: spot/zero-coupon discounting, binomial interest-rate tree, calibration to current yield curve, backward induction, pathwise valuation, Monte Carlo forward-rate simulation, and term-structure models used for bonds with embedded options and rate derivatives.","supports":"Explicitly compares curve-based discounted cashflow pricing, calibrated lattices and path simulation; connects market curve calibration and rate dynamics to embedded-option pricing/hedging.","claim_limit":"One professional curriculum's teaching framework; not an exhaustive catalogue of fixed-income models, calibrated market practice or a universal prerequisite order. Full reading may require candidate access.","search_status":"official 2026 CFA reading page and learning outcomes/summary inspected 2026-10-23"},
    {"source_id":"D011-OPEN-GAMMA-RATES-CURVE-CALIBRATOR","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"OpenGamma Strata project","title":"RatesCurveCalibrator API","url":"https://strata.opengamma.io/apidocs/com/opengamma/strata/pricer/curve/RatesCurveCalibrator.html","source_family":"maintained open-source financial-pricing project documentation / credible grey literature","locator":"Strata API states curve calibration defines reference trades at curve nodes and prices/reprices them using a root finder to fit observed market quotes; supports curve groups, par-spread/present-value calibration measures and stores Jacobians in metadata.","supports":"Practical rate-curve calibration workflow, root-finding and calibration-measure choices for downstream instrument pricing and risk sensitivity.","claim_limit":"API describes Strata's implementation, not the only calibration approach or proof the market inputs/conventions are appropriate. Calibrating a curve to instruments is distinct from validating the resulting model or market-data quality.","search_status":"official OpenGamma Strata API documentation inspected 2026-10-23"},
    {"source_id":"D011-OPEN-GAMMA-PRODUCT-COVERAGE","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"OpenGamma Strata project","title":"Strata Product and Pricing-Model Coverage","url":"https://strata.opengamma.io/product_coverage/","source_family":"maintained open-source financial-pricing project documentation / credible grey literature","locator":"Strata's coverage matrix maps asset classes to supported pricing/risk APIs and models: e.g., Black/normal/SABR for swaptions and caps, Black/VannaÃ¢â‚¬â€œVolga for FX vanilla options, Black/trinomial tree for FX barrier options, CDS, bonds, swaps and multi-curve calibration inputs.","supports":"Implementation-level map of instrument class to model choice and alternatives; illustrates that options depend on product structure and required market data rather than one universal pricing method.","claim_limit":"Documents one platform's supported products and implementations; coverage is not a market-adoption survey, model validation or a recommendation that any one pricer is preferable. Verify API/version for implementation use.","search_status":"official OpenGamma Strata documentation page inspected 2026-10-23"},
    {"source_id":"D011-OPEN-GAMMA-CURVE-CALIBRATION","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"OpenGamma Strata project","title":"Strata Curve Calibration Loader and Market-Instrument Nodes","url":"https://strata.opengamma.io/curve_calibration_loader/","source_family":"maintained open-source financial-pricing project documentation / credible grey literature","locator":"Documentation shows curve groups assigned discount/forward purposes and calibration nodes built from market instruments including OIS/IBOR swaps, basis swaps, futures and deposits; calibration quotes, conventions and tenors are configured as inputs.","supports":"Concrete market-data and instrument basis for bootstrapped multi-curve construction, and how a calibrated curve feeds subsequent pricing calculations.","claim_limit":"Project documentation and selected conventions illustrate implementation; no node set/interpolation is universally appropriate. Market conventions differ by currency, collateral and market period, and calibration fit is not model validation.","search_status":"official OpenGamma Strata documentation inspected 2026-10-23"},
    {"source_id":"D011-QUANTLIBPY-TERM-STRUCTURES-2026","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"QuantLib-Python documentation contributors","title":"QuantLib-Python Term Structures API Reference","url":"https://quantlib-python-docs.readthedocs.io/en/latest/termstructures.html","source_family":"third-party maintained software API documentation / credible grey literature","locator":"Python reference documents yield, volatility, credit and inflation term-structure families and APIs for discount factors, zero rates and forward rates; includes bootstrapped/interpolated curve classes and hazard/survival probability structures.","supports":"Concrete data structures and query outputs used to price and risk-mark derivatives across rates, volatility and credit curves.","claim_limit":"QuantLib's official site identifies this Python reference as a work in progress maintained by an external contributor. Use it as an implementation lead, verify against the official C++ reference/source and independently check the financial conventions and modeling assumptions.","credibility_assessment":"Credible grey technical reference because the maintainer and scope are identifiable and API content is cross-linked to a mature open-source quantitative-finance project. It is expressly labeled work in progress by QuantLib's official documentation page; validate version-specific behavior against the official project manual/source before implementation.","search_status":"Python API reference and QuantLib official documentation caveat inspected 2026-10-23"},
    {"source_id":"D011-QUANTLIB-OPTION-ENGINE-COMPARISON","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"QuantLib open-source project","title":"QuantLib Equity Option Example: Analytic, Finite-Difference, Tree and Monte Carlo Engines","url":"https://github.com/lballabio/QuantLib/blob/master/Examples/EquityOption/EquityOption.cpp","source_family":"maintained open-source quantitative-finance project source code / grey implementation evidence","locator":"Maintainer repository example prices European, Bermudan and American options by changing the attached engine, including finite differences, JarrowÃ¢â‚¬â€œRudd and CoxÃ¢â‚¬â€œRossÃ¢â‚¬â€œRubinstein binomial trees, and Monte Carlo, and reports prices/Greeks for comparison.","supports":"Inspectable implementation comparison showing pricing engine as a replaceable calculation choice for a fixed instrument; helps relate analytic/numerical methods to exercise style and compare estimates/Greeks.","claim_limit":"One library example demonstrates implementation choices and outputs, not the mathematical validity of each method, convergence in every model, or a universal engine selection rule. Verify algorithm assumptions and error controls for the actual product.","credibility_assessment":"Credible first-party open-source implementation evidence from the QuantLib maintainers, with inspectable code and reproducible example context. It establishes what the project implements, not independent model validation or comparative production performance.","search_status":"official QuantLib repository example opened 2026-10-23"},
