    {"source_id":"D011-BIS-CVA-CREDIT-VALUATION-FRAMEWORK","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"Basel Committee on Banking Supervision / Bank for International Settlements","title":"Basel Framework MAR50: Credit Valuation Adjustment Framework","url":"https://www.bis.org/committees/bcbs/basel-framework/standard/mar/50/inforce/2023-01-01/published/2020-07-08","source_family":"prudential regulatory framework","locator":"Current MAR50 page, version effective 1 January 2023, inspected 2026-10-24. MAR50.3 and associated sections define regulatory CVA as adjustment for counterparty credit risk to default-risk-free derivative/SFT values; MAR50.9–50.11 distinguish BA-CVA, SA-CVA (subject to supervisory approval) and specified immateriality treatment. SA-CVA uses regulatory CVA sensitivities to counterparty spreads and market-risk factors; the bank must model exposure and calculate CVA/sensitivities.","supports":"A current regulatory CVA-capital framework that is distinct from accounting/valuation CVA and from counterparty-credit exposure EAD: BA-CVA or approved SA-CVA calculates capital for CVA risk, while SA-CCR/IMM calculates exposure measures.","claim_limit":"Basel prudential capital framework with jurisdictional adoption and in-force-version limits; it is not a universal derivative-pricing recipe or a full accounting-CVA model specification. Check the applicable local rules; the current page's text is regulatory CVA, not a claim that all CVA desks use identical valuation models.","search_status":"Current BIS Basel Framework MAR50 page and §§50.3, 50.9–50.11 inspected 2026-10-24; superseded 2022-effective URL replaced"},
    {"source_id":"D011-BIS-CREDIT-SPREAD-STRUCTURAL-REDUCED-FORM-2004","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"Bank for International Settlements, BIS Working Papers","title":"An Empirical Comparison of Credit Spreads between the Bond Market and the Credit Default Swap Market","url":"https://www.bis.org/publ/work160.pdf","source_family":"central-bank working paper / academic grey literature","locator":"BIS Working Paper 160 (2004), theoretical framework and empirical comparison. Contrasts structural models (firm asset value and default boundary, Merton lineage) with reduced-form/intensity models (default as random stopping time); derives CDS valuation using risk-neutral survival probabilities and discounting, then compares bond and CDS spreads.","supports":"Makes structural and reduced-form credit-model assumptions and outputs explicit and connects risk-neutral default/survival curves to CDS cash-flow pricing.","claim_limit":"Dated working paper and market sample; useful for conceptual model distinctions and historical empirical results, not current market calibration conventions, a universal model ranking, or proof that a single structural/intensity variant is best.","credibility_assessment":"Credible academic grey literature for its bounded conceptual framework and reported historical comparison: BIS identifies the working-paper series, authors, date and research design, and the paper supplies a transparent source trail. It is not itself a prudential rule or peer-reviewed version of record; its dated market results require later research and current contract-source corroboration.","search_status":"BIS paper inspected for model-family definitions, CDS equation and empirical scope 2026-10-23"},
    {"source_id":"D011-BIS-CREDIT-MODEL-CALIBRATION-ERRORS","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"Bank for International Settlements, BIS Working Papers","title":"Measuring Portfolio Credit Risk: Modelling versus Calibration Errors","url":"https://www.bis.org/publications/measuring-portfolio-credit-risk-modelling-versus-calibration-errors","source_family":"central-bank working paper / academic grey literature","locator":"BIS publication abstract for a model-based portfolio-credit-risk study: separates model-specification error from calibration error and reports that flawed calibration can materially affect measured portfolio credit risk in its application.","supports":"Evidence that choosing a model family and calibrating its parameters are separate sources of uncertainty in portfolio credit risk outputs; informs benchmarking and validation needs.","claim_limit":"The source page summarizes one model and dataset; the result does not establish calibration error always dominates specification error, or generalize across portfolios, model families and current market regimes. Inspect the full paper and later evidence before quantitative claims.","credibility_assessment":"Credible as an attributable central-bank research publication with a named study, abstract, classification and inspectable linked paper; its claims are limited to the specific model/data/application summarized. It is research evidence, not regulatory guidance or universal error hierarchy, and should be corroborated with current validation standards and independent studies.","search_status":"BIS publication record and abstract inspected 2026-10-23"},
    {"source_id":"D011-QUANTLIB-REPLACEABLE-PRICING-ENGINES-5","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"QuantLib project; Luigi Ballabio, QuantLib maintainer","title":"QuEP 5: Using Replaceable Pricing Engines in Option Implementations","url":"https://www.quantlib.org/quep/quep005.html","source_family":"maintainer-authored open-source design proposal / credible grey literature","locator":"QuantLib Enhancement Proposal by maintainer Luigi Ballabio describes instrument/pricing-engine separation and analytic, finite-difference and Monte Carlo option engines; argues instrument contracts should be decoupled from calculation implementations and notes engine-specific model structures.","supports":"Implementation architecture showing the same option instrument may be evaluated through distinct pricing engines, clarifying that numerical engine alternatives attach to a pricing problem rather than being separate financial products.","claim_limit":"A project design proposal (not a standard or empirical performance comparison); it describes architecture and examples, not convergence/error rankings, universal engine suitability, or current API completeness.","credibility_assessment":"Credible grey technical material for the proposal's own design rationale and named QuantLib architecture: the author is identifiable as a project maintainer and the project hosts the proposal with code references. It is an engineering design note, not an independent benchmark or finance-method authority; use official current API docs and numerical-analysis research to support engine accuracy or selection.","search_status":"QuantLib Enhancement Proposal inspected 2026-10-23"},
