    {"source_id":"D166-AVSC-ADS-SAFETY-METRICS-2021","domain_id":"D166","publisher":"Automated Vehicle Safety Consortium, SAE Industry Technologies Consortium","title":"Best Practice for Metrics and Methods for Assessing Safety Performance of Automated Driving Systems","url":"https://go.sae.org/rs/525-RCG-129/images/AVSC00006202103.pdf","source_family":"industry-consortium grey literature / voluntary best practice","locator":"AVSC00006202103, issued 2021-03. The document sets out system-level safety metrics and methods, explains the limits of relying only on real-world exposure given rare safety outcomes, and positions predictive indicators alongside outcome metrics. The consortium describes its practices as intended to inform later standards and regulation.","supports":"Safety-performance measurement, evidence interpretation, validation planning and ongoing ADS safety monitoring across development/deployment tasks; supports combining predictive and outcome indicators rather than treating a single metric as a safety proof.","claim_limit":"Voluntary industry best practice, dated 2021, focused on SAE Level 4/5 dedicated vehicles; it is neither a regulator requirement nor independent proof that member practice is safe. Use with current law, current standards, peer-reviewed methods and incident evidence; confirm whether a later AVSC document supersedes a specific recommendation.","credibility_assessment":"Strong grey-literature lead for industry practice because it is an authored consortium deliverable published by SAE ITC, has a dated identifier and explicit scope/rationale, and is designed to inform standards. It remains voluntary, industry-authored and time-bounded; triangulate recommendations against current standards/regulators and independent safety research.","search_status":"official consortium PDF and SAE ITC publisher metadata inspected 2026-10-23"},
    {"source_id":"D166-AVSC-CORE-SAFETY-INFO-2024","domain_id":"D166","publisher":"Automated Vehicle Safety Consortium, SAE Industry Technologies Consortium","title":"AVSC Best Practice for Core Automated Vehicle Safety Information","url":"https://saemobilus.sae.org/reports/avsc-best-practice-core-automated-vehicle-safety-information-avsc-d-02-2024","source_family":"industry-consortium grey literature / voluntary best practice","locator":"AVSC-D-02-2024, published 2024-05-14. SAE abstract says the best practice identifies core safety topics for sharing deployment/use-case-specific ADS safety information, treats safety as continuous across lifecycle stages, and discusses safety metrics and examples.","supports":"Safety-case communication, validation evidence interpretation and lifecycle safety information tasks; complements technical safety analysis with transparent communication to stakeholders.","claim_limit":"The indexed SAE page is an abstract/metadata record, not the full report. It describes an implementation-agnostic voluntary best practice, not a legal duty, full safety-case method, or evidence that any named company is safe. Obtain and inspect the full report before using detailed recommendations.","credibility_assessment":"Credible grey-literature lead because it has a dated AVSC identifier, a traceable SAE ITC publisher, and a defined ADS safety-information scope; the evidence reviewed here is only the official abstract. Treat the summary as discovery/context until the full report and its references are inspected, then triangulate against current regulator, standards and independent research.","search_status":"SAE publisher abstract and AVSC publisher context inspected 2026-10-23; full report not inspected"},
    {"source_id":"D166-APOLLO-SOFTWARE-ARCH-2017","domain_id":"D166","publisher":"Baidu Apollo open-source project","title":"Apollo 2.0 Software Architecture","url":"https://github.com/ApolloAuto/apollo/blob/master/docs/14_Others/Apollo_2.0_Software_Architecture.md","source_family":"maintained open-source project documentation / historical architecture example","locator":"Apollo 2.0 architecture page: describes perception, prediction, routing, planning, control, CAN-bus, HD map and localization modules; gives example data inputs/outputs and notes planning consumes predicted objects, routing, localization and chassis state, while control sends commands to vehicle interface.","supports":"A concrete open implementation example for cross-component boundaries and data handoffs among perception, localization, mapping, planning, control and vehicle integration; useful to test whether proposed area boundaries correspond to distinct functions and outputs.","claim_limit":"This page documents the Apollo 2.0 architecture and explicitly describes a CarOS/ROS implementation; it is historical and platform-specific, not a current universal ADS architecture, curriculum sequence, role taxonomy or safety proof.","credibility_assessment":"Credible as first-party, inspectable architecture documentation from the Apollo project for the named release and software interfaces. Its version age and project-specific design constrain present-day generalization; compare with current Autoware, standards and independent systems research.","search_status":"official Apollo GitHub documentation page opened 2026-10-23; version identified as Apollo 2.0"},
    {"source_id":"D166-APOLLO-LOCALIZATION-MODULE","domain_id":"D166","publisher":"Baidu Apollo open-source project","title":"Apollo Localization Module README","url":"https://github.com/ApolloAuto/apollo/blob/master/modules/localization/README.md","source_family":"maintained open-source project technical documentation","locator":"Current master module README: distinguishes RTK GPS/IMU and multi-sensor-fusion GPS/IMU/LiDAR implementations; lists module inputs and LocalizationEstimate output, components, configuration and implementation extension points.","supports":"Localization/state-estimation function, sensor fusion inputs and output interface; supports a distinct area and shows how GNSS, IMU, LiDAR and maps can complement one another within this particular implementation.","claim_limit":"Apollo's current open-source implementation is one platform example; its available modules do not establish field-wide prevalence, required sensors or universal dependencies. The README has implementation details that may change with the repository revision.","search_status":"official Apollo module documentation opened 2026-10-23"},
    {"source_id":"D166-APOLLO-PLANNING-COMPONENT","domain_id":"D166","publisher":"Baidu Apollo open-source project","title":"Apollo Planning Component Introduction","url":"https://github.com/ApolloAuto/apollo/blob/master/docs/08_Planning/planning_component.md","source_family":"maintained open-source project technical documentation","locator":"Current master component documentation identifies routing, traffic lights, localization, vehicle state, map and prediction as inputs and a trajectory as output. It distinguishes the mature rule-based scenario planner from learning-based modes under exploration and explicitly says E2E mode is not road-tested.","supports":"Planning responsibilities, output consumed by control, and task-specific upstream inputs; supports planning/control separation and helps distinguish deployed or mature implementation from experimental ML modes.","claim_limit":"Apollo implementation documentation, not an industry-wide architecture or proof of road safety. The source itself marks E2E modes as exploratory and not tested on road; do not treat them as production capability.","credibility_assessment":"Credible first-party technical documentation for Apollo's own component interfaces and stated implementation maturity. Project-specific and mutable; independently corroborate general methods and any safety claim. The explicit experimental-status warning narrows the ML claim appropriately.","search_status":"official Apollo documentation page opened 2026-10-23"},
    {"source_id":"D166-APOLLO-CONTROL-TUNING","domain_id":"D166","publisher":"Baidu Apollo open-source project","title":"Apollo Control Parameter Tuning","url":"https://github.com/ApolloAuto/apollo/blob/master/docs/10_Control/how_to_tune_control_parameters.md","source_family":"maintained open-source project technical documentation","locator":"Current master control guide: control consumes planned trajectory and current vehicle status, outputs steering/throttle/brake commands, and describes lateral and longitudinal controllers including an LQR-based lateral controller using a bicycle model.","supports":"Control function, vehicle-dynamics model use and planning-to-actuation interface; supports control as its own technical area and makes the trajectory-following output explicit.","claim_limit":"Apollo-specific implementation and tuning guidance; not a universal controller choice or sufficient evidence of vehicle safety/performance. Controller adequacy depends on vehicle model, operating envelope and validation.","credibility_assessment":"Credible first-party source for the Apollo controller design and tuning procedure documented by its maintainers; version/configuration and platform are specific, so corroborate method theory and performance claims with independent control literature and test evidence.","search_status":"official Apollo documentation page opened 2026-10-23"},
    {"source_id":"D166-ASAM-OPENDRIVE-2026","domain_id":"D166","publisher":"ASAM","title":"ASAM OpenDRIVE 1.9.0: Static Road-Network Description for Simulation","url":"https://www.asam.net/standards/detail/opendrive/","source_family":"industry standards body / technical standard","locator":"Official datasheet lists OpenDRIVE 1.9.0 released 2026-05-19; scope is static road-network description for driving/traffic simulation. The page says OpenDRIVE road networks, OpenCRG road surfaces and OpenSCENARIO dynamic content complement one another for in-the-loop vehicle simulation; the standard is free to view/download.","supports":"HD map data representation and simulation-infrastructure context; explains how static road-network data connects with road surface and dynamic-scenario standards for testing and simulation workflows.","claim_limit":"ASAM standard scope and participating-industry context; it represents a technical exchange format, not a complete map-making task taxonomy, proof of map correctness, safety validation by itself, or legal requirement unless adopted. Verify revision status before implementation.","credibility_assessment":"Strong grey/standards evidence for the standard's own version, scope and intended interoperability: the publisher is a standards organization, version/date are explicit and the specification is publicly inspectable. ASAM describes industry relevance; that claim is not an independent measure of adoption or universal use.","search_status":"official ASAM standard page and current version/date opened 2026-10-23"},
