    {"source_id":"D011-FED-CREDIT-MODEL-COMPARATIVE-ANATOMY-1999","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System, Finance and Economics Discussion Series","title":"A Comparative Anatomy of Credit Risk Models","url":"https://www.federalreserve.gov/econres/feds/a-comparative-anatomy-of-credit-risk-models.htm","source_family":"official central-bank working paper / academic grey literature","evidence_layer":"area_method_context","locator":"Michael B. Gordy, FEDS 1999-28, January 1999; official abstract and linked full paper inspected 2026-10-26. Compares CreditMetrics and CreditRisk+ mathematical structures, identifying functional-form, distributional and approximation differences and using simulation exercises to isolate their effects.","supports":"A direct historical comparison of a structural/threshold portfolio-credit model and an actuarial/factor approach; shows that distinct implementations can share mathematical structures while generating differences from their distributional and computational assumptions.","claim_limit":"Dated research working paper whose disclaimer says views are the author's and findings are preliminary/discussion material. Covers two models, not all structural/reduced-form or modern dependence designs; does not establish current market preference or universal performance.","search_status":"official abstract and linked full paper inspected 2026-10-26","credibility_assessment":"Identifiable central-bank research paper with full text and explicit author-view/preliminary caveat; credible for its comparative analysis, not supervisory policy or current practice prevalence."},
    {"source_id":"D011-SPRINGER-COPULA-CREDIT-PORTFOLIO-MODELING-2015","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"Springer Nature, Innovations in Quantitative Risk Management","title":"Copula-Specific Credit Portfolio Modeling: How the Sector Copula Affects the Tail of the Portfolio Loss Distribution","url":"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-319-09114-3_8","source_family":"peer-reviewed open-access quantitative risk research chapter","evidence_layer":"area_method_context","locator":"Matthias Fischer and Kevin Jakob, conference proceedings chapter first online 2015, pp. 129–145; complete open-access chapter inspected 2026-10-26. Compares CreditMetrics threshold/latent-asset modeling and CreditRisk+ actuarial/factor formulation, Gaussian/t/generalized-hyperbolic/Archimedean/pair-copula alternatives, calibration choices, and impacts on portfolio-loss tails for a hypothetical 5,000-counterparty, 10-sector portfolio.","supports":"Dependence structures are model choices separate from marginal PDs: Gaussian, t, generalized hyperbolic, Archimedean and vine/pair copulas can change joint tail behavior and resulting economic-capital figures; CreditMetrics and CreditRisk+ encode sector dependence differently and their default/loss computation methods differ.","claim_limit":"Hypothetical portfolio and historical PD sample (2003–2012); results depend on calibration, sector mapping, deterministic LGD and paper-specific assumptions. Does not establish universal superiority or a production-ready dependence model. The authors explicitly limit the comparison to default risk even though CreditMetrics also models rating migration.","search_status":"complete open-access Springer chapter inspected 2026-10-26","credibility_assessment":"Peer-reviewed open-access proceedings chapter with full methods, sample, assumptions and limitations inspectable; strong for method comparisons within stated portfolio/model scope, not universal ranking."},
