CURATED_SOURCE_CARDS.extend([
    {"source_id":"D011-RISK-VAR-METHODOLOGY-REVIEW-2013","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"Journal of Securities Operations & Custody / Elsevier","title":"A comprehensive review of Value at Risk methodologies","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.srfe.2013.06.001","source_family":"peer-reviewed finance-methods review","evidence_layer":"area_task_skill_activity","locator":"Publisher search result/abstract inspected 2026-10-24; article published 2013, DOI 10.1016/j.srfe.2013.06.001. Abstract/catalogue describes parametric, nonparametric and semiparametric VaR families, including EVT, filtered historical simulation and CAViaR, and reviews backtesting. Direct publisher access returned 403.","supports":"Discovery map for named VaR estimator families and backtesting procedures to verify through accessible primary studies and current technical documentation.","claim_limit":"Full text was inaccessible in this pass. Its comparative performance summary is not adopted as a finding; source claims are limited to the families identified in the abstract. It is older, so current model families and recent evidence require separate checking.","search_status":"publisher abstract/search result inspected; full text inaccessible (403), 2026-10-24","credibility_assessment":"Peer-reviewed journal review record with identifiable DOI; abstract-level source only. Do not rely on detailed performance conclusions until full text and cited primary comparisons are checked."},
    {"source_id":"D011-DYNAMIC-EVT-VAR-ES-REVIEW-2024","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"Journal of Empirical Finance / Elsevier","title":"An empirical review of dynamic extreme value models for forecasting value at risk, expected shortfall and expectile","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jempfin.2024.101488","source_family":"peer-reviewed financial econometrics review","evidence_layer":"area_task_skill_activity","locator":"Publisher bibliographic page/search result inspected 2026-10-24; Journal of Empirical Finance volume 77, article 101488 (2024). Abstract describes a selective comparison of dynamic threshold-exceedance models for financial-loss VaR, ES and expectile forecasting; reports framework-specific comparative findings. Full article fetch redirected to an inaccessible publisher endpoint.","supports":"Signals a modern tail-risk branch beyond static EVT: dynamic threshold models and framework-dependent forecasts of VaR, ES and expectiles; follow-up primary evidence should inspect methods, samples and metrics.","claim_limit":"Full text is inaccessible in this pass and the review is selective. Do not promote the reported best model family or relative performance as universal; exact assets, forecast periods, threshold choices and scoring rules need inspection.","search_status":"publisher abstract/search result inspected; full text inaccessible, 2026-10-24","credibility_assessment":"Peer-reviewed journal article with publisher metadata and abstract; usable for discovery-level taxonomy but not detailed claims without full text."},
    {"source_id":"D011-ENGLE-MANGANELLI-CAVIAR-2004","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"Journal of Business & Economic Statistics / American Statistical Association","title":"CAViaR: Conditional Autoregressive Value at Risk by Regression Quantiles","url":"https://www.simonemanganelli.org/Simone/Research_files/caviarPublished.pdf","source_family":"peer-reviewed primary financial econometrics paper; full author-hosted article","evidence_layer":"area_method_context","locator":"Engle and Manganelli, JBES 22(4), 367–381 (2004), DOI 10.1198/073500104000000370. Full 15-page published paper inspected 2026-10-26. Sections 2–5 compare prior conditional-volatility, historical-quantile and EVT approaches, define adaptive/indirect/GARCH/symmetric absolute-value CAViaR specifications, estimate conditional VaR directly through regression quantiles and propose the dynamic-quantile test; section 6 applies the method to historical returns.","supports":"A direct conditional-quantile method alternative to estimating a full return distribution or volatility first; the paper connects lagged observations and previous quantiles to a forecast VaR and supplies a model-adequacy diagnostic.","claim_limit":"One 2004 method paper and its empirical sample do not establish best performance, current regulatory approval or universal suitability. CAViaR forecasts VaR quantiles; it is not by itself an ES estimator, credit/liquidity model or complete risk-management framework. Dependence, quantile specification, estimation stability and out-of-sample validation still matter.","search_status":"full published article inspected from author-hosted PDF on 2026-10-26","credibility_assessment":"Peer-reviewed primary article in a statistical methods journal; complete paper and locator inspected. Author-hosted full text is used for method detail; comparative conclusions are limited to the paper's specified models/data."},
    {"source_id":"D011-MCNEIL-FREY-GARCH-EVT-2000","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"Journal of Empirical Finance / authors' research manuscript","title":"Estimation of Tail-Related Risk Measures for Heteroscedastic Financial Time Series: An Extreme Value Approach","url":"https://statmath.wu.ac.at/~frey/publications/evt-garch.pdf","source_family":"peer-reviewed primary financial-risk methods paper; full author-hosted manuscript","evidence_layer":"area_method_context","locator":"McNeil and Frey, Journal of Empirical Finance 7 (2000), 271–300; full 29-page revised manuscript inspected 2026-10-26. Sections 2–4 develop conditional GARCH volatility with EVT threshold modeling of standardized residual tails, conditional VaR and ES estimates, backtesting and multi-day Monte Carlo forecasts.","supports":"A hybrid risk-estimation branch connecting conditional volatility modeling, residual-tail EVT and historical/Monte Carlo methods; it explicitly distinguishes conditional from unconditional tail questions and compares multi-day simulation with naive square-root-of-time scaling in its return samples.","claim_limit":"Historical methods paper with specific assets, model specifications and sample. Its reported improvement over named benchmarks is not a universal performance ranking. GARCH residual assumptions, threshold/sample choice, portfolio composition and backtest design limit transfer; it is not an EVT guarantee for arbitrary dependent tails.","search_status":"full revised manuscript inspected from authors' institution-hosted PDF on 2026-10-26","credibility_assessment":"Peer-reviewed primary journal study with complete author manuscript and methods inspected. The institution-hosted copy supports detailed method review; findings remain sample- and model-specific."},
    {"source_id":"D011-LE-MIDAS-VAR-ES-COMPARISON-2020","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"International Journal of Forecasting / Elsevier","title":"Forecasting value at risk and expected shortfall with mixed data sampling","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0169207020300170","source_family":"peer-reviewed primary forecasting and financial-risk methods paper","evidence_layer":"area_method_context","locator":"Trung H. Le, International Journal of Forecasting 36(4), 1362–1379 (2020), DOI 10.1016/j.ijforecast.2020.01.008. Publisher full-text page and full article abstract inspected 2026-10-26. The empirical design compares 12 models over 43 international equity indices, one-, five- and ten-day horizons, six VaR/ES backtests and two loss functions; benchmarks include historical simulation, GARCH-filtered historical simulation, CAViaR and EVT-based VaR/ES models.","supports":"A directly comparable out-of-sample benchmark showing how VaR/ES estimators, including conditional quantile, filtered historical and EVT branches, can be evaluated at matched forecast horizons with both absolute backtests and relative loss/model-set comparisons. The paper also demonstrates a direct multi-horizon MIDAS route rather than naive aggregation of one-day forecasts.","claim_limit":"The author's abstract reports MIDAS-based methods outperforming listed benchmarks, especially at multi-day horizons, but this is one study across specified international equity indices, sample periods, models and scoring rules. It does not establish universal superiority, transfer to credit/liquidity risk, or the best model for every portfolio. Publisher page is inspected; detailed tables and code were not independently reproduced in this pass.","search_status":"publisher article page/full abstract inspected 2026-10-26; detailed tables/code not independently reproduced","credibility_assessment":"Peer-reviewed primary research article in a forecasting journal; publisher record and detailed abstract support its design and reported findings. Comparative ranking must remain limited to its named assets, periods, benchmarks, tests and losses."},
    {"source_id":"D011-CALMON-ESTABLISHED-VAR-COMPARISON-2021","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"International Statistical Review / International Statistical Institute (Wiley)","title":"An Extensive Comparison of Some Well-Established Value at Risk Methods","url":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/insr.12393","source_family":"peer-reviewed primary statistical-finance comparison; publisher abstract inspected","evidence_layer":"area_method_context","locator":"Calmon, Ferioli, Lettieri, Soares and Pizzinga, International Statistical Review 89(1), 148–166 (2021), DOI 10.1111/insr.12393. Wiley publisher metadata/abstract inspected 2026-10-26. Compares CAViaR, EVT, filtered historical simulation and time-varying higher-order conditional moments using 80 assets and a Monte Carlo simulation.","supports":"Independent multi-asset and simulation comparison evidence for four established VaR method families, complementing Le (2020)'s VaR/ES comparison across explicit one-, five- and ten-day horizons.","claim_limit":"Full article was access-restricted; only publisher abstract and metadata were inspected. The authors report EVT performing best most often and FHS next under their backtests/performance measures, but the method details, asset mix, model configurations and test results were not inspected; do not make this a general ranking or claim of independent replication beyond the stated design.","search_status":"publisher abstract/metadata inspected; full text access restricted 2026-10-26","credibility_assessment":"Peer-reviewed journal comparison with publisher-verified metadata and abstract. Suitable for establishing comparison scope and reporting the authors' bounded summary; detailed technical claims require full text."},
    {"source_id":"D011-FREY-MCNEIL-DEPENDENT-CREDIT-PORTFOLIOS-2002","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"Journal of Banking & Finance / author-hosted ETH Zurich manuscript","title":"VaR and Expected Shortfall in Portfolios of Dependent Credit Risks: Conceptual and Practical Insights","url":"https://statmath.wu.ac.at/people/frey/publications/rome.pdf","source_family":"peer-reviewed primary credit-risk paper; complete author-hosted manuscript","evidence_layer":"area_method_context","locator":"Frey and McNeil, Journal of Banking & Finance 26(7), 1317–1334 (2002); complete 15-page manuscript inspected 2026-10-26. Sections 2–3 examine VaR coherence/non-subadditivity for skewed dependent credit losses and recast CreditMetrics, KMV and CreditRisk+ as Bernoulli-mixture models for comparing portfolio VaR/ES tails.","supports":"A concrete dependence-and-aggregation branch for portfolio credit risk: obligor dependence shapes portfolio-loss tails; the standard model families can be expressed in a common mixture representation to compare VaR/ES; ES is considered when VaR aggregation can mislead.","claim_limit":"Historical theoretical/modeling paper for stylized large balanced/homogeneous credit portfolios and named model versions. It does not cover every modern portfolio-credit model, prove one dependence structure is correct, or establish current software adoption. Large-portfolio approximations and its empirical examples must not be generalized beyond stated assumptions.","search_status":"complete author-hosted manuscript inspected 2026-10-26","credibility_assessment":"Peer-reviewed primary study with full author-hosted paper and locators inspected. Strong for stated model formulation and theoretical example, bounded for modern practice and other portfolio structures."},
    {"source_id":"D011-BIS-FSI-IFRS9-ECL-EXEC-SUMMARY-2017","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"BIS Financial Stability Institute","title":"IFRS 9 and expected loss provisioning: Executive Summary","url":"https://www.bis.org/publications/fsi-summary-ifrs-9-and-expected-loss-provisioning-executive-summary","source_family":"official international supervisory education summary of accounting standard","evidence_layer":"area_method_context","locator":"BIS FSI Executive Summary published 13 December 2017; official page inspected 2026-10-26, sections 'Three stages of impairment' and 'Twelve-month versus lifetime expected credit losses'. Describes Stage 1 12-month ECL, Stage 2 lifetime ECL after significant increase in credit risk, Stage 3 lifetime ECL for credit-impaired exposures, and clarifies that 12-month ECL is lifetime shortfall weighted by default events possible within 12 months.","supports":"A bounded source for the accounting staging/output distinction and why IFRS 9 ECL is not a next-12-month cash-loss forecast or the same output as regulatory credit capital, market VaR or derivative CVA.","claim_limit":"Supervisory summary, not the licensed IFRS 9 standard or a full implementation guide. It focuses mainly on loans and was published in 2017; current accounting policy, scope exceptions, transition issues and jurisdictional adoption require the current standard and local guidance.","search_status":"official BIS FSI summary inspected 2026-10-26; publication date checked","credibility_assessment":"Primary supervisory education summary from BIS FSI, reliable for its stated overview, but secondary to the IASB standard and not sufficient for accounting implementation."},
    {"source_id":"D011-FED-INTERAGENCY-CCR-GUIDANCE-2011","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"Federal Reserve Board, OCC and FDIC","title":"Interagency Supervisory Guidance on Counterparty Credit Risk Management (SR 11-10)","url":"https://www.federalreserve.gov/frrs/guidance/interagency-supervisory-guidance-on-counterparty-credit-risk-management.htm","source_family":"official interagency supervisory guidance","evidence_layer":"area_task_skill_activity","locator":"Guidance issued June 29, 2011 (SR 11-10); official Federal Reserve Regulatory Service page inspected 2026-10-26. Sections III.1–6 and V cover CCR metrics and exposure aggregation, stress tests, CVA valuation/CVA VaR, wrong-way risk, systems, model validation/backtesting; page defines CCR as bilateral exposure before final settlement and CVA as fair-value adjustment for counterparty credit quality.","supports":"Separates counterparty credit exposure/EE and aggregation, market-value CVA, CVA VaR and wrong-way risk; connects market and credit factor dependence, netting/margin, stress testing, horizon choice, model validation and reporting outputs.","claim_limit":"U.S. interagency supervisory guidance for banking organizations, especially large derivatives portfolios; issued in 2011 and not a universal or globally current regulation. Use current Basel/local prudential rules and accounting standards for applicable obligations. It is guidance, not a formula specification for every CCR/CVA implementation.","search_status":"official page and dated sections inspected 2026-10-26","credibility_assessment":"Primary official supervisory guidance, authoritative for the stated U.S. supervisory expectations and dated issuance; scope/jurisdiction and age are explicit."},
    {"source_id":"D011-US-CFR-2026-217-203-MARKET-RISK-APPROACHES","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"U.S. Government Publishing Office / Federal Reserve Board","title":"12 CFR Part 217, Subpart F: Market Risk (2026 annual edition)","url":"https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/CFR-2026-title12-vol2/pdf/CFR-2026-title12-vol2-part217.pdf","source_family":"U.S. federal regulation / official codification","evidence_layer":"area_standards_and_learning","locator":"Official 2026 edition opened 2026-10-24. §§217.203–217.205 (PDF pp. 193–196) cover covered-institution market-risk models: §217.203(6) names variance-covariance, historical simulation and Monte Carlo approaches; §217.203(d) requires initial and ongoing independent validation, conceptual soundness, monitoring, outcome analysis/backtesting and stress testing; §§217.204–217.205 describe VaR capital measure and backtesting.","supports":"Authoritative example of established alternative computational approaches for market-risk measurement, paired with explicit requirements for model choice, risk coverage, portfolio complexity, backtesting, validation and stress testing.","claim_limit":"U.S. Board-regulated banking-institution requirements in the 2026 codification; do not generalize to all financial firms or replace the revised Basel/FRTB capital regime where it applies. The enumerated approaches are permitted examples, not a ranking or claim that they are interchangeable. Verify current consolidated rule and institution applicability before operational use.","search_status":"official 2026 CFR PDF opened; relevant sections inspected 2026-10-24","credibility_assessment":"Primary official U.S. legal codification for its stated entities and edition; authoritative on that text, but jurisdiction- and institution-limited."},
    {"source_id":"D011-BIS-MAR32-BACKTESTING-PLA-CURRENT","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"Basel Committee on Banking Supervision / Bank for International Settlements","title":"Basel Framework MAR32: Internal models approach backtesting and P&L attribution tests","url":"https://www.bis.org/committees/bcbs/basel-framework/standard/mar/32/inforce/2023-01-01/published/2020-03-27","source_family":"international bank prudential standard","evidence_layer":"area_standards_and_learning","locator":"Official current Basel Framework chapter opened 2026-10-24. BIS chapter index lists MAR32 status Current, effective 1 January 2023, last update 27 March 2020. MAR32 defines VaR backtesting and trading-desk P&L attribution tests; desk-level eligibility compares one-day VaR at 97.5% and 99% and tests risk-theoretical versus hypothetical P&L using Spearman correlation and Kolmogorov–Smirnov statistics.","supports":"Explains the regulatory validation branch for banks seeking the internal market-risk models approach and how exception counting and P&L attribution relate to risk-model eligibility; complements, but does not replace, the actual model estimation method.","claim_limit":"Basel standard for adoption by member jurisdictions; implementation and legal effect depend on local transposition. This chapter concerns internal-model regulatory capital eligibility, not a universal backtest for every VaR/ES forecast or investment strategy. ES capital measurement under MAR33 and VaR-based backtesting/PLA under MAR32 are linked regulatory components, not identical outputs.","search_status":"official current framework chapter and status inspected 2026-10-24","credibility_assessment":"Primary Basel Committee standard; authoritative for Basel text and status, with jurisdictional implementation limits."},
    {"source_id":"D011-ISDA-SIMM-METHODOLOGY-V2-7-2025","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"International Swaps and Derivatives Association (ISDA)","title":"ISDA SIMM Methodology, version 2.7+2412 (effective 12 July 2025)","url":"https://www.isda.org/a/DRfgE/ISDA-SIMM_v2.72412_PUBLIC.pdf","source_family":"industry methodology / primary model documentation","evidence_layer":"area_standards_and_learning","locator":"Official public methodology PDF opened 2026-10-24. Cover identifies v2.7 calibrated to December 2024, based on v2.7+2412.1 dated 28 April 2025, effective 12 July 2025. Sections define delta, vega, curvature, inter-curve basis, credit base-correlation and concentration risk treatment; licensing notice restricts use/revision/distribution.","supports":"A current source for how SIMM estimates regulatory initial margin from portfolio sensitivities and risk-factor classifications for in-scope non-cleared derivatives; supports the quantitative-risk area’s model-input, aggregation, implementation and governance coverage.","claim_limit":"Industry model methodology, not a general derivative-pricing method, market-risk VaR/ES model or universal legal requirement. Applicability depends on jurisdiction, counterparty, product and applicable margin rules; ISDA intellectual-property/licensing terms apply. It is one model alternative to the standardised grid approach and requires firm-level authorisation/implementation controls where applicable.","search_status":"official methodology PDF opened and version/effective date checked 2026-10-24","credibility_assessment":"Primary documentation from the model developer; authoritative for the stated SIMM methodology and version, but not independent evidence of performance or universally applicable regulatory status."},
    {"source_id":"D011-EBA-CENTRAL-VALIDATION-ISDA-SIMM-2026","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"European Banking Authority (EBA)","title":"Validation of pro forma initial margin models","url":"https://www.eba.europa.eu/activities/direct-supervision-and-oversight/validation-pro-forma-initial-margin-models","source_family":"EU supervisory authority / regulatory implementation","evidence_layer":"area_standards_and_learning","locator":"Current EBA official page opened 2026-10-24. Explains initial margin for non-centrally cleared OTC derivatives, standardised grid versus model approaches, competent-authority authorisation, and EBA central validation of pro forma model calibration, design, instrument/asset-class/risk-factor coverage and ongoing changes. States ISDA SIMM is currently the only pro forma model in use and the EBA function became ready 1 March 2026.","supports":"Current EU supervisory context for SIMM validation and implementation: model-level central assessment is distinct from counterparty-level authorisation, portfolio mapping, governance and performance assessment.","claim_limit":"EU EMIR scope only; this is not a global rule and does not mean every derivative or counterparty is in scope. EBA model validation does not replace competent-authority authorisation or a firm's own implementation/model-risk work. Check current EMIR text and applicable thresholds for a real entity.","search_status":"official current EBA page opened 2026-10-24","credibility_assessment":"Primary EU supervisory authority source, strong for current mandate and stated scope; specific legal obligations require reading the governing EMIR/RTS provisions and entity-specific applicability."},
    {"source_id":"D011-BIS-UNCLEARED-MARGIN-REVIEW-2025","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"Basel Committee on Banking Supervision / Bank for International Settlements","title":"Review of the implementation of margin requirements for non-centrally cleared derivatives","url":"https://www.bis.org/bcbs/publ/d606.pdf","source_family":"international standard-setting body review / implementation evidence","evidence_layer":"area_standards_and_learning","locator":"Official BIS/BCBS report inspected 2026-10-24. Reports SIMM v2.7 effective 7 December 2024, supplementary mid-cycle recalibration effective 12 July 2025, and third-party model validation concluded April 2025; discusses operational/documentation/collateral implementation around non-centrally cleared derivatives margin requirements.","supports":"Independent policy-level corroboration of SIMM version/currentness and its place within the wider uncleared-margin implementation ecosystem; helps distinguish model methodology, validation, collateral operations and regulatory margin obligations.","claim_limit":"International framework review; it does not make SIMM compulsory in every jurisdiction or counterparty relationship and is not the model's detailed technical specification. Country-level implementation and entity thresholds vary.","search_status":"official BIS/BCBS report inspected 2026-10-24","credibility_assessment":"Primary international standard-setting body report; authoritative for its review statements and framework context, while not a substitute for local law or the ISDA methodology."},
    {"source_id":"D166-ISO-34502-SCENARIO-SAFETY-EVALUATION-2022","domain_id":"D166","publisher":"International Organization for Standardization","title":"ISO 34502:2022 — Scenario-based safety evaluation framework for ADS","url":"https://www.iso.org/standard/78951.html","source_family":"international standard","evidence_layer":"area_standards_and_learning","locator":"Official ISO abstract/status page inspected 2026-10-24. Published 2022-11; applies scenario-based safety evaluation during ADS product development, scoped to ADS as defined in ISO/SAE PAS 22736 and limited-access highways; excludes misuse, HMI and cybersecurity issues.","supports":"Planning and safety-validation context: organize scenario-based safety evaluation of ADS functions, connect ODD/scenario coverage to safety questions and product-development testing.","claim_limit":"Paid standard; abstract-level scope only. Limited-access highway and named ADS-category scope; not a general claim that scenario testing alone proves safety or covers all ODDs. Confirm revision/status before use." ,"search_status":"official ISO abstract/status page inspected 2026-10-24","credibility_assessment":"Primary standards publisher page is authoritative for edition, publication/status and abstracted scope; detailed clauses require the paid standard."},
    {"source_id":"D166-ISO-DIS-34505-SCENARIO-TEST-CASE-GENERATION","domain_id":"D166","publisher":"International Organization for Standardization","title":"ISO/DIS 34505 — Scenario evaluation and test-case generation for ADS","url":"https://www.iso.org/obp/ui?_escaped_fragment_=iso%3Astd%3Aiso%3A34505%3Adis%3Aed-1%3Av1%3Aen","source_family":"draft international standard / committee document","evidence_layer":"area_standards_and_learning","locator":"Official ISO draft page inspected 2026-10-24. Draft scope describes evaluating scenarios and deriving traceable test cases for ADS Level 3 and higher; includes scenario criticality, complexity/frequency, requirement/OD coverage and prioritization of test sets.","supports":"Emerging standards work linking scenario selection/evaluation to executable test cases, objectives, inputs, platform and expected results for ADS verification.","claim_limit":"Draft International Standard, not a published final requirement. Use as emerging direction only; confirm latest ballot/status and do not claim normative compliance or settled requirements.","search_status":"official ISO draft text inspected 2026-10-24","credibility_assessment":"Primary standards-development text is authoritative for its draft scope, but draft content can change and is not a final standard."},
    {"source_id":"D166-UNECE-UNR157-AMENDMENT-4-2023","domain_id":"D166","publisher":"United Nations Economic Commission for Europe","title":"UN Regulation No. 157, Amendment 4: Automated Lane Keeping Systems","url":"https://unece.org/sites/default/files/2024-07/R157am4e_1.pdf","source_family":"official UN vehicle type-approval regulation text","evidence_layer":"area_standards_and_learning","locator":"UNECE Amendment 4 text inspected from official PDF listing 2026-10-24; covers ALKS approval including system safety/fail-safe response, HMI, object/event detection and response, data storage, cybersecurity/software updates, track and public-road tests, with dedicated traffic-disturbance critical scenarios.","supports":"Conditional type-approval context for ALKS planning, control, scenario testing, fallback/fail-safe behavior and system integration; useful to distinguish bounded ALKS regulation from general ADS research.","claim_limit":"UN Regulation type-approval scope is ALKS and depends on contracting-party adoption and amendments; not a universal AV design standard or a blanket rule for all autonomy levels, vehicles or jurisdictions.","search_status":"official UNECE amendment PDF inspected 2026-10-24","credibility_assessment":"Primary regulatory text from UNECE; jurisdictional adoption and current amendment series still need checking for a specific vehicle market."},
])

