CURATED_SOURCE_CARDS.extend([
    {"source_id":"D011-HUSMANN-SHIVAROVA-STEINERT-SPARSITY-STABILITY-MINVAR-2022","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"Springer Nature / Risk Management","title":"Sparsity and stability for minimum-variance portfolios","url":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1057/s41283-022-00091-0","source_family":"peer-reviewed open-access empirical portfolio-method comparison","evidence_layer":"area_method_context","locator":"Full open-access article inspected 2026-10-27. Compares maximum-likelihood/sample, linear shrinkage, nonlinear shrinkage and POET covariance inputs in rolling minimum-variance portfolio experiments using S&P 500 daily returns; 319 surviving stocks and a 100-stock subset, 504-day estimation windows and 4,778 out-of-sample trading days. Tests LASSO sparsity and an explicit turnover constraint. Reports that LASSO alone may increase turnover when tuning changes each period, while adding turnover control reduces it. The data retain survivorship bias by selecting stocks present throughout 1998–2018; daily frequency, homoscedasticity, tuning choices and one market/universe constrain transfer.","supports":"Same-sample comparison showing covariance estimator, portfolio sparsity, constraints, tuning/rebalancing and turnover costs interact; demonstrates a failure case for assuming that LASSO alone controls turnover and compares covariance inputs under a disclosed rolling design.","claim_limit":"One equity universe and daily, long historical window with survivorship selection; its 504-day rolling setup and hyperparameter choices are study design, not universal recommendations. Results do not establish a general ranking across asset classes, mandates, intraday strategies or live portfolios.","search_status":"full open-access text inspected 2026-10-27","credibility_assessment":"Peer-reviewed original empirical article with disclosed data construction, rolling evaluation, estimator/constraint comparisons and limitations. Strong for a bounded same-sample comparison, not universal adoption or performance claims."},
    {"source_id":"D011-DOM-HOWARD-JANSEN-LOHRE-BEYOND-GMV-2025","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"Quantitative Finance / Lancaster University EPrints","title":"Beyond GMV: the relevance of covariance matrix estimation for risk-based portfolio construction","url":"https://eprints.lancs.ac.uk/id/eprint/228677/","source_family":"peer-reviewed open-access portfolio construction study","evidence_layer":"area_method_context","locator":"Lancaster accepted manuscript and abstract inspected 2026-10-27; peer-reviewed Quantitative Finance 25(3), 403–419 (2025). Equity portfolio study contrasts unconstrained global minimum variance (GMV) with constrained GMV and risk parity. Its abstract reports that long-only, asset-level constraints plus a transaction-cost penalty can yield results similar to shrinkage-based covariance estimators even using sample covariance; it also reports time-series return dynamics remain relevant for volatility reduction.","supports":"Shows covariance-estimator performance depends on portfolio objective and implementation constraints; directly connects covariance inputs, long-only/asset constraints, risk-parity versus GMV objectives and transaction-cost penalties.","claim_limit":"Equity and risk-based portfolio scope; the repository record supplies the accepted version and abstract. Does not establish results for return-seeking, liability-driven or cross-asset mandates, nor a universal sample-covariance recommendation.","search_status":"accepted version and repository abstract inspected 2026-10-27","credibility_assessment":"Repository identifies a refereed, published journal article and supplies an open accepted manuscript. Evidence supports the study's scoped comparison, not a general rule."},
    {"source_id":"D011-CHAN-KARCESKI-LAKONISHOK-COVARIANCE-PORTFOLIO-1999","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"National Bureau of Economic Research / Review of Financial Studies","title":"On Portfolio Optimization: Forecasting Covariances and Choosing the Risk Model","url":"https://www.nber.org/papers/w7039","source_family":"peer-reviewed empirical paper and NBER working-paper record","evidence_layer":"area_method_context","locator":"NBER working-paper record and abstract inspected 2026-10-27; published version in Review of Financial Studies (Winter 1999). Evaluates covariance forecasts and out-of-sample optimized-portfolio volatility for stock-return risk models. The abstract reports a few factors capture general covariance structure, more factors did not improve forecast power, and models produced similar portfolio outcomes overall; larger differences appeared under tracking-error volatility, where benchmark-attribute matching was favorable.","supports":"Historical counterevidence to assuming more factors or a statistically richer covariance model necessarily improves portfolio outcomes; demonstrates that model ranking depends on forecast loss versus a portfolio/benchmark objective.","claim_limit":"Abstract-level evidence in this pass; older stock-market sample and model set. It cannot rank current estimators, asset classes or transaction-cost-aware implementations.","search_status":"official NBER record and abstract inspected 2026-10-27","credibility_assessment":"NBER primary research record links the paper to its published peer-reviewed version and states the empirical comparison. Abstract-only claims are kept at that level."},
    {"source_id":"D011-SCIKIT-LEARN-COVARIANCE-LEDOITWOLF-1-9-1-2026","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"scikit-learn documentation","title":"LedoitWolf covariance estimator, scikit-learn 1.9.1","url":"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/generated/sklearn.covariance.LedoitWolf.html","source_family":"official versioned software API documentation","evidence_layer":"area_implementation_context","locator":"Official stable API documentation inspected 2026-10-27; documentation identifies version 1.9.1, estimator inputs and returned covariance/location/precision/shrinkage attributes, and cites the 2004 Ledoit-Wolf formula. It exposes fit data assumptions, covariance error norms and the assumption that test observations are drawn from the same distribution as fitted observations.","supports":"Version-pinned implementation entry point for linear covariance shrinkage and its available output fields and statistical assumptions; supports reproducible comparison of a named implementation rather than treating method name as version-independent.","claim_limit":"General-purpose statistical Python API, not a portfolio optimizer or finance-specific validation protocol. Current stable documentation can change; API availability does not establish suitability, financial-data handling, numerical performance or live use.","search_status":"official stable documentation inspected 2026-10-27","credibility_assessment":"Maintainer documentation is authoritative for the documented API/version and stated assumptions, not for investment performance or methodological superiority."},
    {"source_id":"D011-CRAN-POET-2-0-IMPLEMENTATION-2026","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"CRAN / POET package authors","title":"POET: Principal Orthogonal Complement Thresholding, version 2.0","url":"https://stat.ethz.ch/CRAN/web/packages/POET/index.html","source_family":"official R package registry and versioned statistical-software documentation","evidence_layer":"area_implementation_context","locator":"CRAN package record and reference manual/search-indexed documentation inspected 2026-10-27. POET 2.0 estimates large covariance matrices under an approximate factor model by thresholding principal orthogonal complements. The manual exposes factor-count K, thresholding choices (soft, hard, SCAD, adaptive LASSO), covariance/correlation thresholding, and covariance/factor/loading outputs; it warns that underestimating K can perform very badly and says K is user-determined with guidance from eigenvalues or Bai–Ng selection.","supports":"Named, versioned factor-plus-sparse-residual covariance implementation and its inputs, assumptions, alternatives and outputs; explains why POET is structurally different from generic linear shrinkage.","claim_limit":"CRAN record dates version 2.0 to 2016; package availability is not evidence of active release cadence or current production adoption. Factor assumptions and tuning/threshold choices must fit the data; manual guidance is not a universal K-selection guarantee.","search_status":"CRAN package record and indexed manual inspected 2026-10-27; direct package page fetch had a cache miss","credibility_assessment":"CRAN is authoritative for the package/version and authors' submitted documentation; direct record metadata and indexed manual were checked, but the package page itself could not be fetched in this pass."},
    {"source_id":"D011-QIAO-BU-GIBBERD-LIAO-WEN-LI-TIMEVARYING-COSTS-2023","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"Journal of Empirical Finance / Lancaster University EPrints","title":"When ‘time varying’ volatility meets ‘transaction cost’ in portfolio selection","url":"https://eprints.lancs.ac.uk/id/eprint/205305/1/JEF_final_copy.pdf","source_family":"peer-reviewed empirical method paper; author-hosted full text","evidence_layer":"area_method_context","locator":"Author-hosted full text inspected 2026-10-27; Journal of Empirical Finance 73 (2023), 220–237. Proposes a mean-variance approach that detects covariance change points and rebalances only when detected, explicitly trading off use of recent information against excessive trading; abstract reports out-of-sample comparisons on variance, turnover and Sharpe ratio with transaction costs.","supports":"A complementary dynamic covariance/rebalancing design where detection governs when to trade; makes covariance forecasting, change detection, rebalance frequency and transaction costs an integrated but distinct design choice.","claim_limit":"One proposed empirical strategy and its authors' specified evaluation; does not establish that change-point-triggered rebalancing dominates fixed schedules or suits every market, cost model or mandate.","search_status":"author-hosted full text inspected 2026-10-27; publisher page was access-limited","credibility_assessment":"Peer-reviewed journal paper with full author-hosted text inspected. The publisher abstract was accessible, while the author-hosted copy provides the method detail; preserve the study-specific scope."}
])
CURATED_SOURCE_CARDS.extend([
    {"source_id":"D011-TIMMERMANN-FORECASTING-METHODS-FINANCE-2018","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"Annual Review of Financial Economics","title":"Forecasting Methods in Finance","url":"https://www.annualreviews.org/content/journals/10.1146/annurev-financial-110217-022713","source_family":"peer-reviewed methods review","evidence_layer":"area_method_context","locator":"Timmermann (2018), Annual Review of Financial Economics 10:449–479; publisher page and abstract inspected 2026-10-27. Reviews mean, variance and return-distribution forecasts, low signal-to-noise, persistent predictors, instability and forecast evaluation under model-search/data-mining risk.","supports":"Conceptual map separating expected-return, variance and distribution forecasts and the distinct problem of evaluating selected models; supports treating forecasts as inputs to a mandate-specific portfolio decision.","claim_limit":"Review published in 2018; it is not a current asset-class-by-asset-class ranking, software guide or proof that any forecast is useful out of sample.","search_status":"publisher abstract/page inspected 2026-10-27","credibility_assessment":"Peer-reviewed Annual Review synthesis by an established finance forecasting scholar; useful for method taxonomy and known limitations, not for current tool/version facts."},
    {"source_id":"D011-GOYAL-WELCH-ZAFIROV-EQUITY-PREMIUM-2022-LOOK-2024","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"Review of Financial Studies / Oxford Academic","title":"A Comprehensive 2022 Look at the Empirical Performance of Equity Premium Prediction","url":"https://academic.oup.com/rfs/article/37/11/3490/7749383","source_family":"peer-reviewed empirical forecast replication and extension","evidence_layer":"area_method_context","locator":"Goyal, Welch and Zafirov (2024), Review of Financial Studies 37(11), 3490–3557; full publisher-hosted text inspected 2026-10-27. Reexamines 29 predictors from 26 newer papers plus 17 earlier predictors, extending data through December 2021; reports that more than one-third of newer predictors lost in-sample significance and about half of remaining significant predictors had poor out-of-sample performance. It distinguishes in-sample, extended-sample, out-of-sample and hypothetical portfolio utility checks.","supports":"Long-horizon equity-premium evidence that return predictors can decay and that statistical significance, out-of-sample forecast performance and investor utility are separate evaluation targets; gives an explicit reason to retain simple baselines and research-history/multiple-testing controls.","claim_limit":"Broad U.S. equity-premium prediction review/replication through 2021, not a cross-asset forecast leaderboard. Authors note that including original discovery periods makes the replication tests not independent or maximally harsh; results depend on predictor definitions, sample extension and selected hypothetical strategies.","search_status":"full publisher-hosted article inspected 2026-10-27","credibility_assessment":"Peer-reviewed RFS article with extensive replication, sample-extension and out-of-sample analysis; strong primary synthesis for its stated U.S. equity-premium scope, not universal evidence against all return prediction."},
    {"source_id":"D011-CAMPBELL-THOMPSON-RETURN-PREDICTION-2008","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"Review of Financial Studies / Harvard DASH repository","title":"Predicting Excess Stock Returns Out of Sample: Can Anything Beat the Historical Average?","url":"https://dash.harvard.edu/entities/publication/73120378-7dd1-6bd4-e053-0100007fdf3b","source_family":"peer-reviewed empirical forecast-method paper; institutional repository copy","evidence_layer":"area_method_context","locator":"Campbell and Thompson (2008), Review of Financial Studies 21(4), 1509–1531; Harvard record and author article inspected 2026-10-27. Reports that weak sign restrictions on predictive-regression coefficients and forecasts can improve out-of-sample results over the historical-average benchmark, with small but economically meaningful explanatory power in their U.S. stock-return study; valuation-model restrictions can further improve results in their tests.","supports":"A conditional, constrained-regression alternative to unrestricted predictor regression and historical-average baselines; shows how prior economic sign restrictions, forecast horizon and out-of-sample evaluation connect to portfolio utility.","claim_limit":"One U.S. aggregate-stock forecasting study and its sample/specification; it does not establish universal predictability or transfer to other assets, markets, forecast horizons or costs.","search_status":"Harvard record and linked author article inspected 2026-10-27","credibility_assessment":"Peer-reviewed RFS article; the Harvard institutional record identifies the published version and provides an accessible copy. Claims are limited to the paper's sample and restrictions."},
    {"source_id":"D011-SALCHER-RISK-ADJUSTED-FORECAST-ERROR-2026","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"Journal of Forecasting / Wiley","title":"Lost in Translation? Risk-Adjusting RMSE for Economic Forecast Performance","url":"https://doi.org/10.1002/for.70134","source_family":"peer-reviewed open-access forecast-evaluation method paper","evidence_layer":"area_method_context","locator":"Salcher et al. (2026), Journal of Forecasting 45(5), 2587–2605; full open-access article inspected 2026-10-27. Links mean and covariance forecast errors to Sharpe-ratio gaps across 13 strategies; tests 100,000 Monte Carlo paths calibrated to Fama–French 12 industry portfolios over 1964–2023 and an empirical application. Finds ordinary RMSE is a restricted proxy and less aligned with economic performance than risk-adjusted error measures; covariance-error terms matter more in volatile conditions and for some portfolio strategies.","supports":"Method link between expected-return/volatility forecasts and downstream portfolio objectives; distinguishes statistical forecast loss from economic/portfolio performance and compares complementary mean and covariance error components across strategies.","claim_limit":"The empirical calibration uses U.S. industry portfolios and the primary simulation assumes normal iid returns; Monte Carlo results are conditional on the model and empirical data. The measures are not universal forecast-selection rules or evidence of live profit.","search_status":"full open-access publisher text inspected 2026-10-27","credibility_assessment":"Peer-reviewed open-access methods article with disclosed simulation design and empirical application; evidence is strong for the forecast-metric/portfolio-objective relationship within its tested scope."},
    {"source_id":"D011-YU-CHIOU-LEE-LIN-RETURN-FORECAST-PORTFOLIO-OBJECTIVES-2020","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"International Review of Economics & Finance / Elsevier","title":"Portfolio models with return forecasting and transaction costs","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1059056018309614","source_family":"peer-reviewed empirical portfolio-method comparison","evidence_layer":"area_method_context","locator":"Yu, Chiou, Lee and Lin (2020), International Review of Economics & Finance 66, 118–130; publisher metadata and abstract inspected 2026-10-27 (direct fetch returned 403). Compares return-forecast-enhanced mean-variance, Omega, linearized VaR, CVaR and downside-risk portfolio models with transaction costs and optimized short-selling weights, using daily international-ETF returns over 14 years. Abstract reports higher improvement for mean-variance/Omega than loss-control objectives, forecast gains not caused by lower transaction costs, and variation across market-volatility conditions.","supports":"Direct, mandate/objective-sensitive comparison of return forecasts within portfolio construction, including return-volatility tradeoff versus tail/downside-loss objectives, equal-weight benchmark, rebalancing and transaction-cost considerations.","claim_limit":"Publisher abstract only in this pass; no full text or exact parameter/horizon sensitivity inspected. One international ETF sample and study-specific costs; do not infer universal objective ranking or forecast advantage.","search_status":"publisher metadata/abstract inspected 2026-10-27; direct page fetch returned HTTP 403","credibility_assessment":"Peer-reviewed journal article with clear empirical sample and objective comparison in publisher abstract. Access limitation means claims stay at abstract scope."}
])
