CURATED_SOURCE_CARDS.extend([
    {"source_id":"D011-WIECKI-CAMPBELL-LENT-STAUTH-888-ALGORITHMS-OOS-2016","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"The Journal of Investing / Quantopian authors","title":"All That Glitters Is Not Gold: Comparing Backtest and Out-of-Sample Performance on a Large Cohort of Trading Algorithms","url":"https://doi.org/10.3905/joi.2016.25.3.069","source_family":"peer-reviewed practitioner-dataset study of out-of-sample validation","evidence_layer":"area_method_context","locator":"Wiecki, Campbell, Lent and Stauth (2016), The Journal of Investing 25(3), 69–80; full author manuscript inspected 2026-10-27 from a hosted copy, with DOI and journal record cross-check. Studies 888 Quantopian US-equity algorithms written in 2015, point-in-time versioned, backtested over 2010–2015 using minute data and a minimum 6–12 months true OOS. Uses Zipline simulation with splits, costs, order delays, liquidity, impact and slippage; tests 5-fold CV for ML hyperparameter selection plus a 20% holdout. Reports weak IS-to-OOS predictive value for single common metrics, evidence that more backtest days correlate with larger Sharpe shortfall, and a holdout ML model to rank expected OOS performance.","supports":"A concrete research protocol joining point-in-time strategy versioning, trial/backtest history, train/CV/holdout separation, simulated execution costs and subsequent OOS performance; shows evaluation metrics and repeated strategy testing need context beyond in-sample Sharpe.","claim_limit":"One proprietary platform cohort and US-equity universe, authored by platform employees, filtered to 888 algorithms with only 6–12 months minimum OOS; observed correlations are modest and regime shift may contribute. It does not establish causal effects, universal CV design, broad market adoption or live execution results.","search_status":"full author manuscript inspected 2026-10-27; DOI/journal record cross-checked","credibility_assessment":"Peer-reviewed Journal of Investing empirical study with unusually rich point-in-time practitioner data and disclosed validation design. Platform-generated sample and author affiliation create selection/context limits; use as a bounded practice example, not neutral market census."},
    {"source_id":"D011-ARPM-ML-QUANTFIN-CERTIFICATION-CURRICULUM-2026","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"ARPM","title":"Certification in Machine Learning for Quantitative Finance: curriculum and professional learning sequence","url":"https://www.arpm.co/certification/","source_family":"professional curriculum and learning-resource provider","evidence_layer":"area_task_skill_activity","locator":"Official ARPM programme page inspected 2026-10-27. Lists complementary Machine Learning courses (Mathematical Statistics for Finance, Mean-Covariance Learning, Probabilistic ML, Time Series and Reinforcement Learning) and Quantitative Finance courses (Financial Engineering, Portfolio and Enterprise Risk Management, Portfolio Construction and Trading); says tracks may be followed in either order/individually by background and objectives. It describes primers, code, exercises, homework and instructor-reviewed practical projects; course sequence and next start date reflect page state at access date.","supports":"A current professional curriculum example that places statistical/ML methods beside financial engineering, risk, portfolio construction and trading, using project-based implementation; demonstrates these are teachable, connected branches rather than evidence of a single universal prerequisite order.","claim_limit":"One provider's commercial certification curriculum and self-reported programme statistics; not an independent survey of employer practice, a university degree, accreditation standard, or a universal route. Course page claims about career impact are marketing and not adopted as outcomes evidence.","search_status":"official programme page opened and inspected 2026-10-27","credibility_assessment":"Primary source for the provider's current curriculum, sequencing options and learning format. Commercial provider; claims about market recognition, career outcomes and breadth are not independently verified."},
    {"source_id":"D011-OLAGOKE-LLM-TRIAL-ADJUSTED-OUTSAMPLE-GATE-2026","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"SSRN / independent researcher","title":"Trials-adjusted Out-of-Sample Gating for LLM-proposed Trading Strategies","url":"https://papers.ssrn.com/sol3/papers.cfm?abstract_id=7361818","source_family":"working paper / early LLM-assisted strategy-validation protocol","evidence_layer":"area_method_context","locator":"Olagoke (2026), SSRN working paper posted 2026-08-29; publisher-indexed full-paper text/abstract inspected 2026-10-27, direct PDF fetch returned 403. Describes an LLM proposer separated from deterministic verifier, walk-forward OOS gate, acceptance threshold that rises with candidate count, and rolling-window/parameter robustness sweeps. Reports a small U.S. equity case study with Alpaca daily bars, 32–83 symbols and data ending 2026-07-17; its selected strategies failed the stated robustness gate.","supports":"A specific preliminary example where an LLM increases candidate-generation throughput, while existing selection-bias/multiple-testing principles require counting trials and applying frozen, walk-forward validation; illustrates possible deterministic gate architecture and reported failure cases.","claim_limit":"Unreviewed single-author SSRN preprint with short 2–4 year U.S. large-cap sample, author-designed system and no independent replication; abstract/publisher-index text only because direct full-text fetch failed. Its proposed acceptance rule is not a validated universal guarantee or evidence of autonomous profitable trading. No distinct inferential correction is established beyond disclosed trial counting, walk-forward testing and robustness checks.","search_status":"SSRN record and indexed full-text excerpt inspected 2026-10-27; PDF fetch returned HTTP 403","credibility_assessment":"Transparent but unreviewed grey literature from an independent author. Useful as a discovery/prototype record only; results and protocol need peer review, full reproducibility materials and independent replication before stronger claims."}
])
