    {"source_id":"D011-BROADIE-GLASSERMAN-MC-GREEKS-1996","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"INFORMS / Management Science; Columbia University author manuscript","title":"Estimating Security Price Derivatives Using Simulation","url":"https://pubsonline.informs.org/doi/10.1287/mnsc.42.2.269","source_family":"peer-reviewed primary computational-finance paper / publisher abstract and author manuscript","evidence_layer":"area_method_context","locator":"Broadie and Glasserman, Management Science 42(2) (1996), pp.269-285; publisher abstract and Columbia-hosted full paper opened 2026-10-24.","supports":"Introduces pathwise and likelihood-ratio Monte Carlo estimators for security-price derivatives and compares them with finite-difference resimulation; examples include European, Asian and stochastic-volatility options.","claim_limit":"Estimator conditions differ: pathwise differentiation requires sufficient payoff regularity, while likelihood-ratio weighting relies on suitable transition-density regularity and may have variance tradeoffs. The study's examples do not establish a universal estimator ranking.","credibility_assessment":"Primary peer-reviewed Management Science paper with publisher abstract and author-hosted paper; strong for the named estimators and tested examples, bounded for generalization."},
    {"source_id":"D011-FOURNIE-MALLIAVIN-MC-GREEKS-1999","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"Finance and Stochastics / Springer; indexed abstract via RePEc","title":"Applications of Malliavin Calculus to Monte Carlo Methods in Finance","url":"https://ideas.repec.org/a/spr/finsto/v3y1999i4p391-412.html","source_family":"peer-reviewed primary stochastic-finance paper / indexed publisher metadata and abstract","evidence_layer":"area_method_context","locator":"Fournié, Lasry, Lebuchoux, Lions and Touzi, Finance and Stochastics 3(4) (1999), pp.391-412; abstract/indexed record opened 2026-10-24; full Springer text restricted.","supports":"Uses integration-by-parts/Malliavin weights to represent price sensitivities for multidimensional diffusion models and discontinuous path-dependent payoffs; abstract illustrates exotic European options and comparison with Monte Carlo finite differences.","claim_limit":"Abstract-only. Formula conditions, weight construction, variance, implementation and comparison details require full text; diffusion setting should not be generalized automatically to jump models or all payoffs.","credibility_assessment":"Peer-reviewed primary journal article with scholarly metadata and abstract; supports the stated method scope, with access-limited technical details."},
    {"source_id":"D011-GILES-GLASSERMAN-SMOKING-ADJOINTS-2005","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"Oxford University Research Archive; Michael Giles and Paul Glasserman","title":"Smoking Adjoints: Fast Evaluation of Greeks in Monte Carlo Calculations","url":"https://ora.ox.ac.uk/objects/uuid%3Af536b1a8-c988-4bda-9c5f-a322652139fd","source_family":"primary computational-finance technical report / university author manuscript","evidence_layer":"area_method_context","locator":"Giles and Glasserman technical report (2005; related Risk article 2006); Oxford archive abstract and manuscript record opened 2026-10-24.","supports":"Reverse-mode adjoint propagation computes pathwise sensitivities backward and can reduce cost for a small number of outputs with many input risk factors; the paper demonstrates LIBOR market-model sensitivities.","claim_limit":"A technical report/manuscript; speed gains depend on input/output dimensions and implementation. Adjoint pathwise differentiation inherits differentiability/regularity conditions and does not itself solve discontinuous-payoff Greeks.","credibility_assessment":"Author-hosted primary technical report from Oxford and Columbia researchers; credible for algorithm and demonstrated example, not independent evidence of universal production performance."},
    {"source_id":"D011-CAPRIOTTI-GILES-AAD-REVIEW-2024","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"Quantitative Finance; author manuscript hosted by Oxford Mathematical Institute","title":"15 Years of Adjoint Algorithmic Differentiation in Finance","url":"https://www2.maths.ox.ac.uk/~gilesm/files/AAD_Review.pdf","source_family":"peer-reviewed review/tutorial author manuscript / institutional research resource","evidence_layer":"area_method_context","locator":"Capriotti and Giles (2024), Quantitative Finance; full 28-page author manuscript opened 2026-10-24, including tutorial, applications to Monte Carlo/PDE and calibration, and payoff-regularity limitations.","supports":"A broad technical tutorial/review of AAD use for financial sensitivities, risk, Monte Carlo/PDE pricing and calibration, with method history and computational scaling considerations.","claim_limit":"Review synthesis is not a standardized implementation or proof that AAD is best for every payoff, model, derivative order or input/output shape; check the underlying primary results and regularity assumptions.","credibility_assessment":"Expert review by researchers with primary contributions to AAD in finance, full manuscript inspected; use as a map and corroboration, not a substitute for method-specific source evidence."},
    {"source_id":"D011-OPENGAMMA-AAD-CALIBRATION-IFT","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"OpenGamma Quantitative Research; Marc Henrard","title":"Adjoint Algorithmic Differentiation: Calibration and Implicit Function Theorem","url":"https://quant.opengamma.io/Adjoint-Algorithmic-Differentiation-OpenGamma.pdf","source_family":"specialist quantitative-finance technical note / credible industry grey literature","evidence_layer":"area_method_context","locator":"Henrard, OpenGamma technical PDF, full text opened 2026-10-24; sections on calibrated parameter sensitivities and implicit differentiation of solver equations.","supports":"Shows how adjoint derivatives can propagate through a calibrated pricing model using the implicit function theorem, without differentiating the root-finding algorithm itself, to obtain full input-to-price sensitivities.","claim_limit":"Specialist technical note, not independent peer-reviewed comparison or proof that every calibration map is differentiable/well-conditioned; singular/ill-conditioned Jacobians and implementation conventions remain important.","credibility_assessment":"Named quantitative-finance specialist and technical research note from an industry pricing-software organization; useful for implementation context, with vendor/practitioner scope and no independent benchmark."},
    {"source_id":"D011-TONG-LIU-IMPORTANCE-SAMPLING-GREEKS-2016","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"INFORMS Journal on Computing","title":"Importance Sampling for Option Greeks with Discontinuous Payoffs","url":"https://pubsonline.informs.org/doi/10.1287/ijoc.2015.0674","source_family":"peer-reviewed primary computational-finance paper / publisher full abstract","evidence_layer":"area_method_context","locator":"Tong and Liu, INFORMS Journal on Computing 28(2) (2016), pp.223-235; publisher abstract opened 2026-10-24.","supports":"Proposes importance sampling around a thickened payoff-boundary hypersurface to estimate discontinuous-payoff Greek contributions that pathwise differentiation misses; derives an unbiased expectation representation under the method's construction.","claim_limit":"Abstract-level details; the method's boundary geometry, thickness limit, variance and payoff requirements must be checked in the paper. It is not a generic control variate or universally stable estimator.","credibility_assessment":"Primary peer-reviewed INFORMS article with publisher abstract; credible for the proposed estimator and stated tests, bounded for broader implementation."},
    {"source_id":"D011-CHAN-JOSHI-DISCONTINUOUS-GREEKS-2013","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"Mathematical Finance / Wiley","title":"Fast Monte Carlo Greeks for Financial Products with Discontinuous Pay-offs","url":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/j.1467-9965.2011.00509.x","source_family":"peer-reviewed primary computational-finance paper / publisher abstract","evidence_layer":"area_method_context","locator":"Chan and Joshi, Mathematical Finance 23(3) (2013), pp.459-495; Wiley full abstract opened 2026-10-24.","supports":"Introduces Brownian-process discretization schemes that permit pathwise Greek estimation for selected trigger/discontinuous payoff products in the LIBOR market model, under a one-dimensional proxy-constraint condition.","claim_limit":"The scheme depends on its proxy-constraint and discretization assumptions; it is not a general method for arbitrary payoff discontinuities or models. Full algorithmic implementation details require article access.","credibility_assessment":"Primary peer-reviewed Mathematical Finance article and publisher abstract; supports its described class and conditions, not generic applicability."},
    {"source_id":"D011-SCOLERI-BIANCHETTI-KUCHERENKO-QMC-AAD-2021","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"Wilmott / Wiley","title":"Application of Quasi Monte Carlo and Global Sensitivity Analysis to Option Pricing and Greeks: Finite Differences vs. AAD","url":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/wilm.10972","source_family":"peer-reviewed computational-finance study / publisher abstract","evidence_layer":"area_method_context","locator":"Scoleri, Bianchetti and Kucherenko, Wilmott 2021(116), pp.66-83; Wiley abstract opened 2026-10-24.","supports":"Compares MC/QMC sampling and finite-difference versus adjoint algorithmic differentiation for selected option prices/Greeks; abstract reports QMC-FD matching AAD accuracy for tested cases with a small number of Greeks and less implementation effort.","claim_limit":"Abstract-level, representative instruments and study configurations; does not establish universal QMC or FD superiority, especially for more Greeks, discontinuous payoffs or different models.","credibility_assessment":"Peer-reviewed specialist computational-finance study with publisher abstract; credible for its comparison's reported scope, with full experimental details not inspected."},
    {"source_id":"D011-GILES-VIBRATO-MC-GREEKS-2009","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"Monte Carlo and Quasi-Monte Carlo Methods 2008 / Springer; Oxford author manuscript","title":"Vibrato Monte Carlo Sensitivities","url":"https://people.maths.ox.ac.uk/~gilesm/files/mcqmc08.pdf","source_family":"peer-reviewed computational-finance conference chapter / full author manuscript","evidence_layer":"area_method_context","locator":"Giles, in Monte Carlo and Quasi-Monte Carlo Methods 2008, Springer (2009), pp.369-382; full Oxford-hosted author manuscript opened 2026-10-24.","supports":"A hybrid Greek estimator applies pathwise sensitivity to earlier path evolution and a likelihood-ratio derivative to a conditional final-step expectation, extending pathwise estimation to discontinuous payoffs; the paper reports a variance-reduction modification and compatibility with adjoints for multiple first-order sensitivities.","claim_limit":"Variance orders and computational comparisons rely on the paper's timestep, payoff and model assumptions. This method is not automatically applicable to every path dependence or discontinuity; verify conditional distributions and discretization.","credibility_assessment":"Primary author manuscript for a peer-reviewed Springer computational-finance chapter; full text inspected, strong for algorithm description and reported examples, bounded for generalization."},
