D166_PLANNING_RELATIONSHIP_NOTES = [
    {
        "topic":"Mission route, behavior choice, path and time-based trajectory are related stages with different outputs",
        "finding":"A destination and map support a mission/global route. Local planning then uses route/map context with current localized vehicle state and perceived/predicted agents to select or revise behavior (such as follow, yield, lane-change or stop), construct a path/drivable region, and generate a time-indexed trajectory with position, speed and acceleration for control. Validation/diagnostics can inspect candidate and selected trajectories before control consumes them. This is a common modular pattern, not an immutable universal stack: some architectures combine behavior and trajectory generation, and receding-horizon/MPC planners may include control decisions directly. Teach the terms by each stage's input/output and handoff, not as synonyms.",
        "source_ids":["D166-AUTOWARE-PLANNING-DESIGN-2026","D166-APOLLO-PLANNING-COMPONENT-CURRENT","D166-HYBRID-MOTION-PLANNING-SURVEY-2024"],
        "claim_limit":"Autoware/Apollo are two implementation examples and the hybrid survey describes modular patterns; architectures differ and some planners merge layers. Do not turn this typical flow into a hard universal sequence or graph blocker without checking the target design.",
        "role_task_relevance":[{"role_title":"software developer","relevance":"conditional planning stack implementation and interface task","condition":"Applies to developer assignments implementing behavior, path/trajectory generation or planning interfaces; role title alone does not imply these tasks.","role_source_ids":["D166-AURORA-BEHAVIOR-PLANNING-ROLE-2026-10-23","D166-DIDI-MOTION-PLANNING-ROLE-2026-10-23"]}],
    },
    {
        "topic":"Search, sampling, optimization and interpolation are method families for different planning subproblems; hybrid combinations are common",
        "finding":"Graph/search methods (including A*/Dijkstra and lattice-style search) explore discretized or structured candidate spaces; sampling methods (for example RRT-family approaches) explore candidate states/paths; optimization methods shape a feasible path or trajectory against costs and constraints; splines/interpolation can represent or smooth candidate geometry. Vehicle kinematics/dynamics, collision checking, map/drivable bounds, traffic rules, agent uncertainty, timing and compute budget change which method is suitable. These families can be alternatives when solving the same defined path/trajectory task, but hybrid systems may use search or sampling to produce candidates and optimization to refine/rank them. None is a universal prerequisite for another, and a method label without its representation, constraints and objective is not enough to choose.",
        "source_ids":["D166-APOLLO-PLANNING-PLUGIN-CATALOG","D166-AUTOWARE-PLANNING-DESIGN-2026","D166-ADS-PATH-PLANNING-SURVEY-2024","D166-HYBRID-MOTION-PLANNING-SURVEY-2024","D166-SAMPLING-PLANNING-REVIEW-2024"],
        "claim_limit":"The ADS path-planning survey is abstract-level and the sampling review is broader robotics; employer postings show named methods at particular teams. This supports taxonomy and conditional examples, not comparative rankings or universal industry prevalence.",
        "role_task_relevance":[{"role_title":"software developer","relevance":"conditional method implementation choice","condition":"Waabi, Gatik and Torc postings name different algorithm families for their specialist planning work; choose based on the assigned vehicle model, ODD and planner contract, not the title alone.","role_source_ids":["D166-WAABI-MOTION-PLANNING-ROLE-2026-10-23","D166-GATIK-MOTION-PLANNING-ROLE-2026-10-23","D166-TORC-MOTION-PLANNING-ROLE-2026-10-23"]}],
    },
    {
        "topic":"Rule-based, learned and end-to-end planning differ in what they learn and where the safety/trajectory contracts sit",
        "finding":"Rule/state-machine planners explicitly encode scenario logic and behavior; learning-based planners may learn behavior or trajectory proposals from demonstrations (imitation learning), optimize policies through reinforcement learning, or learn selected components such as prediction, costs, candidate ranking or perception-to-planning representations. End-to-end methods may map sensor inputs directly to motion/planning outputs, reducing some module boundaries while making interpretation, attribution and validation harder. Interactive prediction-planning goes beyond a one-way predicted-agent input by modeling how surrounding agents react to the AV's candidate behavior. Hybrid designs can combine learned proposals or predictions with analytic constraints, optimization, rule logic and independent checks. The required dependency is the actual data/output contract of that design, not a generic claim that machine learning always follows a planner or replaces it.",
        "source_ids":["D166-APOLLO-PLANNING-COMPONENT-CURRENT","D166-HYBRID-MOTION-PLANNING-SURVEY-2024","D166-INTERACTIVE-PREDICTION-PLANNING-SURVEY-2026","D166-E2E-DRIVING-SURVEY-2024"],
        "claim_limit":"Apollo explicitly labels its documented E2E mode exploratory/not road-tested; surveys describe research and future directions. Do not claim production readiness, safety or superiority from this evidence. Prediction is a distinct upstream input unless a joint architecture models reciprocal interaction.",
        "role_task_relevance":[{"role_title":"software developer","relevance":"conditional planning/ML integration task","condition":"Aurora's posting asks for motion-planning/ML integration into verifiable systems; use as a specific employer example, not a general prerequisite for planning work.","role_source_ids":["D166-AURORA-BEHAVIOR-PLANNING-ROLE-2026-10-23"]}],
    },
    {
        "topic":"Safety constraints and planner evaluation complement planning algorithms and do not certify the whole vehicle",
        "finding":"Formal safety assumptions/models such as the proposed RSS rules and IEEE 2846's scoped assumptions can inform behavior constraints or safety-related planner checks, but neither replaces perception/prediction quality, vehicle dynamics, implementation assurance or a complete system safety case. Open-loop trajectory similarity and closed-loop simulation answer different questions: closed-loop evaluation lets the proposed trajectory alter ego state and may include reactive agents, collisions, road compliance, progress and comfort; simulation still cannot establish real-world safety by itself. A useful planner comparison therefore records scenario coverage, agent reactivity, map/sensor assumptions, controller, metrics, compute and real-world validation boundaries.",
        "source_ids":["D166-RSS-ORIGINAL-PAPER-2017","D166-IEEE-2846-BEHAVIOR-SAFETY","D166-MOBILEYE-RSS-PROPOSAL","D166-NUPLAN-OFFICIAL-BENCHMARK","D166-NUPLAN-DEVKIT-EVALUATION","D166-AVSC-ADS-SAFETY-METRICS-2021"],
        "claim_limit":"RSS is a proponent-originated model; IEEE summary explicitly excludes uncertainty sources and states it does not guarantee whole-system safety. nuPlan is a bounded research simulator and NHTSA's 2017 voluntary guidance is not a current comprehensive approval regime. No source here establishes a safety guarantee for a specific AV.",
    },
]

