    {"source_id":"D011-CBN-STRESS-GUIDELINE-2019","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"Central Bank of Nigeria","title":"Guideline on Stress Testing for the Nigerian Banks (March 2019)","url":"https://www.cbn.gov.ng/Out/2019/BSD/Guideline%20on%20Stress%20Testing%20%20for%20Nigerian%20Banks.pdf","source_family":"official national banking supervisory guideline","evidence_layer":"area_method_context","locator":"Full 30-page guideline inspected 2026-10-27. §§3.1–3.3 and 3.8–3.10 (paras. 10–23, 37–45): sensitivity and macroeconomic scenarios, bank-specific/risk-specific and dynamic tests, capital/liquidity and asset-category shocks, simultaneous risk effects, reverse stress tests, asset-liquidity/funding linkages; includes Nigeria oil-price exposure context.","supports":"A Nigerian banking application of scenario and stress testing: select material bank-specific and macroeconomic risks, connect shocks to credit/market/operational exposures and capital/liquidity outcomes, and examine interactions and management responses.","claim_limit":"Issued and effective March 2019; this source establishes the published guideline and its stated scope, not its unamended current legal status or every later CBN transposition. It is high-level supervisory design guidance, not a single scenario-generation estimator; it does not prescribe market microstructure measures for every risk or portfolio.","credibility_assessment":"Primary CBN supervisory guideline, inspected in full. Strong for the issued Nigerian banking expectations and named scenario scope; verify later amendments before asserting current legal status."},
    {"source_id":"D011-CBN-LIQUIDITY-ILAAP-GUIDELINE-2021","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"Central Bank of Nigeria","title":"Guidelines on Liquidity Risk Management and ILAAP (2021)","url":"https://www.cbn.gov.ng/Out/2021/BSD/6.%20GUIDELINES%20ON%20LIQUIDITY%20RISK%20MANAGEMENT%20AND%20ILAAP.pdf","source_family":"official national banking supervisory guideline; official search excerpt inspected, direct fetch limited","evidence_layer":"area_method_context","locator":"Official CBN search-indexed excerpts inspected 2026-10-27; the PDF direct fetch returned 403. The excerpt identifies §§4.5 and 4.8, paras. 85–108: HQLA buffers, contractual/non-contractual cash flows, bank-specific and market-wide liquidity scenarios, market/funding-liquidity linkage, multiple horizons including intraday, and use in contingency funding plans.","supports":"A source lead for Nigerian bank liquidity-risk stress practices and conditional linkage between market liquidity and funding liquidity; retain the described claims at the indexed-excerpt level until the full guideline is accessible.","claim_limit":"The full PDF was not inspected in this pass; claims are limited to CBN-indexed excerpts. Issue date/amendment/current legal status must be confirmed from the official document and any later circular before regulatory-current assertions.","credibility_assessment":"Primary source publisher, but only indexed text was accessible during this check. Suitable for locating and bounding the stated provisions, not for detailed interpretation beyond the visible excerpt."},
    {"source_id":"D011-CBN-OIL-SHOCK-STRESS-SVAR-2021","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"Central Bank of Nigeria Economic and Financial Review","title":"Are Nigerian Banks Vulnerable to Oil Price Shocks? A Stress Test Approach","url":"https://dc.cbn.gov.ng/efr/vol59/iss2/4/","source_family":"CBN-hosted peer-reviewed applied banking stress-test study; abstract inspected","evidence_layer":"area_method_context","locator":"Odu and Sanusi, Economic and Financial Review 59(2), 61–88 (2021); official repository article page and abstract inspected 2026-10-27. Abstract reports an SVAR-X study using 2007Q1–2020Q4 data and baseline/adverse/severely adverse oil-price scenarios to examine deposit-money-bank non-performing loans; the authors state data limits prevented extending the analysis to capital adequacy.","supports":"A bounded Nigerian applied example linking macroeconomic oil-price shocks, SVAR-X scenario analysis and bank asset-quality outcomes; it demonstrates one concrete risk-specific design and output rather than a universal banking stress model.","claim_limit":"Only the publisher-hosted abstract and metadata were inspected, not the 28-page article. The study period ends in 2020 and the abstract explicitly reports a capital-adequacy data limitation; do not infer current banking vulnerability, full model specification or generalizability beyond its sample/design.","credibility_assessment":"CBN-hosted journal article with named authors, volume, pages and DOI; useful primary applied-study evidence at abstract level, not a full-text methodological review."}
