    _risk_methods_established = area_matrix_claim_mappings[("D011:quantitative-financial-risk-modeling", "methods_and_designs", "established/traditional")]
    _risk_methods_established["claim_mappings"].append({
        "claim":"VaR estimators are alternatives with different assumptions, not a single ladder: variance-covariance uses a specified distribution and estimated moments; historical simulation resamples observed risk-factor changes; Monte Carlo generates scenarios from a specified model; filtered historical simulation rescales historical residuals by current conditional volatility; EVT focuses on tail behavior. Current CFA curriculum compares the first three and states there is no single right method; RBA's full-text worked example demonstrates that distribution assumptions can change estimates; Bank of England research examines filtered-simulation conditionality and procyclicality. The evidence supports task- and portfolio-specific comparison, not a universal ranking.",
        "locator":"CFA Institute 2026 Measuring and Managing Market Risk learning outcomes/summary; RBA RDP 9708 sections 2-3; Bank of England Working Paper 525 (2015); Abad and Benito, Mathematics and Computers in Simulation (2013) publisher record.",
        "source_ids":["D011-CFA-2026-MEASURING-MANAGING-MARKET-RISK","D011-RBA-VAR-METHODS-AND-BACKTESTING-1997","D011-BOE-FILTERED-HISTORICAL-SIMULATION-2015","D011-RISK-VAR-METHODOLOGY-REVIEW-2013"],
        "area_context":"This is the core established market-risk estimation branch; it sits beside stress/scenario analysis and backtesting, and does not cover credit, liquidity, operational or model risk by itself.",
        "claim_limit":"CFA is a current curriculum source, RBA is a historical Australian banking example, and the Bank of England and publisher records have paper-specific empirical scope. The source set explains assumptions and comparisons but does not rank methods for every instrument, nonlinear portfolio, horizon or data regime.",
        "relationship_context":"Portfolio positions and risk-factor data feed valuation and the selected loss-distribution method; the resulting VaR forecast is then compared with aligned realized P&L in backtesting. Stress/scenario analysis probes shocks beyond historical sampling, while ES requires tail-severity methods and tests beyond VaR exception counts. These branches complement each other; one method is not prerequisite to another."
    })
    _risk_methods_established["claim_mappings"].append({
        "claim":"Bank stress testing is designed around material exposures and scenarios, not a universal asset-class checklist or one estimator. The CBN guideline names risk-specific and dynamic scenario testing, macroeconomic and asset-category shocks, simultaneous risk effects, reverse stress tests, and links between asset-market liquidity, funding liquidity and valuation under stress. Its Nigerian scope includes oil-price, FX, rates, equity/asset-value and bank-specific shocks. A CBN-hosted study gives one bounded application: SVAR-X scenarios for oil-price shocks and bank NPLs, while stating that data did not support a capital-adequacy extension. Market microstructure/liquidity-adjusted measures are therefore relevant when the task concerns liquidity, executable depth, liquidation or funding interaction; they are not general prerequisites for credit ECL, operational-risk capital or every risk scenario.",
        "locator":"CBN Guideline on Stress Testing for the Nigerian Banks (March 2019), §§3.1–3.3 and 3.8–3.10, paras. 10–23 and 37–45; Odu and Sanusi (2021), CBN Economic and Financial Review 59(2), abstract; existing BOJ L-VaR and ECB market/funding-liquidity evidence; BIS WP1250 full text §§2–4 for market-specific microstructure indicators.",
        "source_ids":["D011-CBN-STRESS-GUIDELINE-2019","D011-CBN-OIL-SHOCK-STRESS-SVAR-2021","D011-BOJ-LIQUIDITY-ADJUSTED-VAR-2000","D011-ECB-MARKET-FUNDING-LIQUIDITY-2023","D011-BIS-ML-MARKET-STRESS-2025"],
        "area_context":"This closes the traditional stress-scope question for banking risk modeling: scenarios are selected from material institution/portfolio exposures and risk interactions. It adds Nigerian banking context without making that jurisdiction universal; specific market-liquidity measures remain a conditional branch inside risk modeling.",
        "claim_limit":"The CBN guideline is dated March 2019; this source confirms its published text and scope, not whether it has been amended after issue. The CBN study is abstract-level evidence, has a 2007–2020 sample and states a data limitation for capital adequacy. The BOJ and ECB evidence has jurisdiction- and model-specific scope. None supplies an exhaustive catalogue of asset-class stress algorithms or makes microstructure measures mandatory for all risk work.",
        "relationship_context":"Exposure and risk identification determine scenario variables; scenarios feed valuation, credit-loss and liquidity projections; joint market/funding stresses reveal interactions. Market depth and liquidation measures enter when a market-liquidity or liquidation task requires them. Stress outcomes then inform capital/liquidity review and management actions; the scenario design complements VaR/ES rather than replacing its estimators or validation."
    })
