            _covariance_packet_sources = ["D011-HUSMANN-SHIVAROVA-STEINERT-SPARSITY-STABILITY-MINVAR-2022", "D011-DOM-HOWARD-JANSEN-LOHRE-BEYOND-GMV-2025", "D011-CHAN-KARCESKI-LAKONISHOK-COVARIANCE-PORTFOLIO-1999", "D011-SCIKIT-LEARN-COVARIANCE-LEDOITWOLF-1-9-1-2026", "D011-CRAN-POET-2-0-IMPLEMENTATION-2026", "D011-QIAO-BU-GIBBERD-LIAO-WEN-LI-TIMEVARYING-COSTS-2023"]
            _covariance_packet_log = "D011-QSYSTEM-COVARIANCE-ESTIMATOR-CONSTRAINT-COST-CHECK-20261027"
            _systematic_techniques_current["claim_mappings"].append({
                "claim":"Covariance-estimator choice cannot be ranked apart from the portfolio objective and implementation design. A same-sample rolling equity comparison evaluates sample/maximum-likelihood, linear and nonlinear shrinkage, and POET estimates with GMV allocation, changing LASSO sparsity and an explicit turnover constraint; in that design LASSO alone can increase turnover as its tuning changes, while a separate turnover constraint controls it. A 2025 risk-based portfolio study reports that long-only asset limits and transaction-cost penalties can reduce the apparent advantage of alternative covariance estimators under constrained GMV/risk parity, although time-series dynamics remain useful for volatility. Historical stock-model evidence likewise cautions that additional factors need not improve covariance forecasts or optimized outcomes, while benchmark tracking error can alter the ranking. Thus estimator, objective (GMV, risk parity, benchmark-aware), constraints, forecast horizon, rebalance rule and costs interact; they are task-specific complements/alternatives rather than a single prerequisite ladder.",
                "locator":"Husmann, Shivarova and Steinert (2022), full open text, rolling S&P 500 design and conclusion; Dom et al. (2025), peer-reviewed Quantitative Finance article, Lancaster accepted manuscript/repository abstract; Chan, Karceski and Lakonishok (1999), NBER record and abstract; scikit-learn stable LedoitWolf 1.9.1 API; CRAN POET 2.0 record/manual; Qiao et al. (2023), author-hosted Journal of Empirical Finance full text. Inspected 2026-10-27.",
                "source_ids":_covariance_packet_sources,
                "area_context":"After defining the investable universe, risk objective and data horizon, the learner can compare a sample covariance baseline with shrinkage, factor-residual/POET or time-varying estimators; then test each within the chosen allocation objective and constraints. A GMV/risk-parity learner should assess out-of-sample risk, turnover and costs under the actual rebalance schedule. A return-seeking or benchmark-relative portfolio also needs expected-return/benchmark design; covariance selection does not replace forecasting or strategy definition. The named scikit-learn and POET APIs are reproducible implementation examples with different assumptions, not mandatory tools.",
                "claim_limit":"The strongest same-design estimator/constraint comparison uses surviving S&P 500 constituents, daily returns, 504-observation rolling estimates and study-specific tuning over 1998–2018; it retains survivorship bias, assumes homoscedasticity and does not cover other asset classes. The 2025 study's accessible repository evidence is the abstract, and the 1999 comparison is abstract-level historical stock evidence. scikit-learn documents a general-purpose estimator; POET 2.0 is an older package and its factor-count/threshold choices require validation. The 2023 event-triggered rebalancing paper is one proposed empirical design. No study supports a universal winner, horizon, rebalance frequency, factor count or production-adoption claim.",
                "relationship_context":"Point-in-time returns/universe + chosen objective/benchmark -> estimate covariance (sample OR linear/nonlinear shrinkage OR factor structure such as POET; time-varying model where dynamics matter) -> combine with mean/forecast inputs only for objectives that use them + constraints (long-only, concentration, benchmark, sparsity) -> optimize target weights -> decide rebalance schedule (fixed or covariance-change-triggered) -> measure realized risk, turnover, costs and benchmark error. Estimators are alternatives at the covariance-input stage; portfolio constraints and turnover controls are complementary, and the latter can change estimator rankings. Outputs then feed execution/TCA and risk review, while strategy/forecast design remains a separate upstream input."
            })
            _systematic_techniques_current["evidence_ids"] = list(dict.fromkeys(_systematic_techniques_current["evidence_ids"] + _covariance_packet_sources))
            _systematic_techniques_current["search_log_ids"] = list(dict.fromkeys(_systematic_techniques_current["search_log_ids"] + [_covariance_packet_log]))
            _forecast_packet_sources = ["D011-TIMMERMANN-FORECASTING-METHODS-FINANCE-2018", "D011-GOYAL-WELCH-ZAFIROV-EQUITY-PREMIUM-2022-LOOK-2024", "D011-CAMPBELL-THOMPSON-RETURN-PREDICTION-2008", "D011-SALCHER-RISK-ADJUSTED-FORECAST-ERROR-2026", "D011-YU-CHIOU-LEE-LIN-RETURN-FORECAST-PORTFOLIO-OBJECTIVES-2020", "D011-LEUSHUIS-PETKOV-REALIZED-VOL-FORECASTING-REVIEW-2026", "D011-JFUTURES-GARCH-IMPLIED-REALIZED-2016", "D011-ARCH-VOLATILITY-PROCESSES-2026"]
            _forecast_packet_log = "D011-QSYSTEM-RETURN-VOLATILITY-FORECAST-OBJECTIVE-COMPARISON-20261027"
            _systematic_techniques_current["claim_mappings"].append({
                "claim":"Return, variance and distribution forecasts are distinct model outputs, and their usefulness depends on the downstream objective and evaluation. Long-sample U.S. equity-premium evidence shows predictor performance can decay out of sample; Campbell–Thompson's sign-restricted regressions provide a conditional alternative to an unrestricted regression/historical-average baseline. Volatility forecasts form separate branches (ARCH/GARCH-family, option-implied, realized-volatility and combinations) with horizon- and backtest-dependent evidence. Across 13 portfolio strategies, a 2026 study finds ordinary RMSE can misstate economic forecast value: expected-return error is central for portfolios that use mean forecasts, while covariance error gains importance in turbulent conditions and for some diversification strategies. A 14-year daily international-ETF study contrasts forecast-enhanced return/volatility objectives (mean-variance and Omega) with VaR/CVaR/downside objectives and reports different gains by objective and volatility regime, without attributing them to lower trading costs. The learner should compare a simple benchmark, forecast target/horizon and cost-aware portfolio utility under the selected mandate rather than treating statistical accuracy as portfolio value.",
                "locator":"Timmermann (2018) Annual Review of Financial Economics; Goyal, Welch and Zafirov (2024) RFS full text, predictor extension through 2021; Campbell and Thompson (2008) RFS / Harvard repository copy; Salcher et al. (2026) Journal of Forecasting full text, 13 portfolio strategies and Fama–French 12-industry calibration; Yu et al. (2020) International Review of Economics & Finance publisher abstract, 14-year daily international ETFs; Leushuis and Petkov (2026) realized-volatility review and Kambouroudis et al. (2016) GARCH/implied/realized comparison already indexed. Inspected 2026-10-27.",
                "source_ids":_forecast_packet_sources,
                "area_context":"After choosing instrument universe, target and decision horizon, a learner may estimate expected-return signals, conditional variance/correlation, or a return distribution; these are complementary inputs only when the selected objective consumes them. Mean-variance/Omega objectives trade return against volatility, GMV/risk parity emphasize risk allocation, and VaR/CVaR/downside objectives emphasize loss-tail criteria. Each branch then compares forecasts to simple baselines and evaluates portfolio outcomes with relevant risk, benchmark, utility, turnover and transaction costs. Covariance estimation is one input-stage choice; it does not substitute for expected-return forecasting, allocation objective, signal selection or execution.",
                "claim_limit":"Evidence is contrasted, not a single common-data leaderboard: U.S. aggregate equity-premium predictors through 2021, U.S. industry-portfolio-based simulation/empirical forecast evaluation, and daily international ETFs over 14 years. The 2024 RFS extension includes original discovery periods and is not an independent replication; Campbell–Thompson evidence is one U.S. sample; the ETF evidence is publisher-abstract scope. The volatility comparison sources cover specified stocks and horizons. None establishes universal forecast superiority, investor utility across markets, or a prescribed sequence for every systematic mandate.",
                "relationship_context":"Point-in-time data + task/mandate + forecast horizon -> (expected-return model OR conditional-volatility/correlation model OR distribution/quantile model; combine only as the portfolio objective requires) -> objective branch (return-risk, minimum-risk/risk-parity, benchmark-aware, or tail/downside) + constraints -> weights/holdings -> objective-matched out-of-sample forecast and portfolio evaluation -> turnover/transaction-cost and execution checks. Statistical forecast loss and portfolio economic value are related but not interchangeable; covariance and mean forecasts are complementary inputs for some strategies, while risk-only/diversification strategies can use other structures."
            })
            _systematic_techniques_current["evidence_ids"] = list(dict.fromkeys(_systematic_techniques_current["evidence_ids"] + _forecast_packet_sources))
            _systematic_techniques_current["search_log_ids"] = list(dict.fromkeys(_systematic_techniques_current["search_log_ids"] + [_forecast_packet_log]))
