    _control_foundation_claim={"claim":"Vehicle-motion control builds on vehicle axes/equations of motion, longitudinal/lateral dynamics, tire slip/force generation and friction limits, steering/actuator response, and estimation of states not measured directly. The needed model fidelity depends on speed, slip, combined braking/cornering, vehicle and operating domain.","locator":"University of Zagreb 2025/26 course schedule/outcomes; Autoware MPC model options; inspected 2026-10-28.","source_ids":["D166-UNIZAGREB-VEHICLE-DYNAMICS-CONTROL-2025-26","D166-AUTOWARE-MPC-LATERAL-CONTROLLER","D166-MATHWORKS-VEHICLE-DYNAMICS-BLOCKSET-R2026B"],"area_context":"Foundations for converting planned paths/speeds into motion commands and choosing a vehicle model for the actual operating regime.","claim_limit":"Sources do not set a universal threshold for kinematic versus dynamic models; it depends on vehicle, tire, ODD and validation evidence.","relationship_context":"Trajectory/speed + estimated pose/velocity/actuator state -> select suitable vehicle/tire model -> compute steering/longitudinal command -> validate tracking, stability and comfort. Detailed suspension/powertrain knowledge is conditional on whether it affects the controlled plant."}
    _control_method_claim={"claim":"A trajectory plus odometry/steering feedback enters lateral and longitudinal controllers, which emit steering and velocity/acceleration commands. Autoware documents Pure Pursuit or MPC for lateral tracking and PID for longitudinal tracking; Apollo documents an LQR lateral implementation. They are axis- and interface-specific alternatives, not a single prerequisite ladder.","locator":"Autoware trajectory follower, Pure Pursuit, MPC and PID API/design pages; Apollo control tuning guide; inspected 2026-10-28.","source_ids":["D166-AUTOWARE-TRAJECTORY-FOLLOWER-CONTROL","D166-AUTOWARE-PURE-PURSUIT-CONTROLLER-CURRENT","D166-AUTOWARE-MPC-LATERAL-CONTROLLER","D166-AUTOWARE-PID-LONGITUDINAL-CONTROLLER","D166-APOLLO-CONTROL-TUNING"],"area_context":"Control methods after trajectory/state inputs and vehicle dynamics; selection depends on axis, model, speed/curvature, actuator delay, constraints, compute period and validation target.","claim_limit":"Project docs establish implementations, not independent ranking or road safety. Actual interface, model/tuning and target hardware are vehicle-specific.","relationship_context":"Planner trajectory + estimated state -> choose lateral and longitudinal methods for the subproblem -> emit motion commands -> vehicle interface converts them to available actuators -> validate tracking, comfort, stability, timing and fallback."}
    _control_tool_claim={"claim":"Control work uses an AV software stack (Autoware/Apollo controller modules/interfaces) together with vehicle and simulation environments such as MATLAB/Simulink Vehicle Dynamics and MPC toolboxes. The latter provide configurable tire/actuator models, calibration, closed-loop simulation and HIL workflows; these tools complement software controllers but do not validate a real vehicle by themselves.","locator":"Autoware and Apollo controller documentation; MathWorks R2026b Vehicle Dynamics/MPC toolboxes; University of Zagreb 2025/26 course; inspected 2026-10-28.","source_ids":["D166-AUTOWARE-TRAJECTORY-FOLLOWER-CONTROL","D166-APOLLO-CONTROL-TUNING","D166-MATHWORKS-VEHICLE-DYNAMICS-BLOCKSET-R2026B","D166-MATHWORKS-MPC-TOOLBOX-R2026B","D166-UNIZAGREB-VEHICLE-DYNAMICS-CONTROL-2025-26"],"area_context":"Tools support model design, tuning, simulation/SIL/HIL and integration after the vehicle interface and plant are defined; open stack and licensed tools differ in access and capability.","claim_limit":"Vendor/project pages do not establish comparative performance or universal adoption; simulator fidelity, license, version and hardware matter.","relationship_context":"Controller/model -> configure implementation and simulation -> run closed-loop scenarios/tune against tracking, stability, comfort and timing -> HIL/vehicle evaluation where available -> preserve model/version/log assumptions."}
    _control_learning_claim={"claim":"A representative learning path moves from vehicle/tire dynamics and feedback-control foundations through models/state estimation and controller design, then adds feature- and jurisdiction-specific safety standards. A course supplies learning outcomes; a standard sets system/project requirements, not a personal credential by itself.","locator":"University of Zagreb 2025/26 course; ISO 26262:2018 and ISO 21448:2022 records; ISO 23792-1:2026 and ISO/FDIS 19484 status/preview; checked 2026-10-28.","source_ids":["D166-UNIZAGREB-VEHICLE-DYNAMICS-CONTROL-2025-26","D166-ISO-26262","D166-ISO-21448","D166-ISO-23792-1-2026-MCS","D166-ISO-19484-FDIS-HMCS-2026"],"area_context":"Functional safety, SOTIF and motorway-chauffeur requirements enter after controller/system context is known; they have different scopes. ISO 23792-1/19484 apply to specific motorway features, while ISO 26262/SOTIF address broader safety processes/functions.","claim_limit":"One course is illustrative. ISO 26262:2018 and ISO 21448:2022 are under revision; ISO/FDIS 19484 is not final. SOTIF excludes well-established dynamic-stability systems. No source makes these universal role credentials.","relationship_context":"Dynamics/control -> interface and feature design -> applicable hazard/ODD/safety requirements -> tests/validation. Standards constrain and contextualize work; they do not substitute for methods or prove safety."}
    _control_frontier_claim={"claim":"Emerging control includes model-residual learning around MPC/iLQR/MPPI, learned local policies and end-to-end/foundation-model systems that may emit trajectories or direct actuation. Evidence spans Autoware experiments, racing/off-road demonstrations and research reviews; public-road transfer and safe production use remain unestablished.","locator":"Autoware Smart MPC docs; IAC racing papers; CMU-RI-TR-25-55; 2026 foundation-model and end-to-end reviews; inspected 2026-10-28.","source_ids":["D166-AUTOWARE-SMART-MPC-LEARNING-CONTROL","D166-ICRA-LMPC-ERROR-DYNAMICS-IAC-2024","D166-SONG-ETAL-IAC-FBL-MPC-2026","D166-CMU-OFFROAD-RL-VEHICLE-CONTROL-2025","D166-FOUNDATION-MODELS-ADS-SURVEY-2026","D166-E2E-ADS-ARCHITECTURES-CONTROL-REVIEW-2026"],"area_context":"Optional research branch after model-based controller, vehicle dynamics and closed-loop simulation foundations; residual learning, local policy and direct actuation are different designs with different interfaces.","claim_limit":"Racing/off-road results are ODD-specific; reviews/prototypes do not show public-road safety, certification or universal performance gains.","relationship_context":"Nominal model/controller -> collect representative data or define policy -> train residual/local policy -> simulate for robustness/constraints -> HIL/vehicle evaluation and safety monitoring. If the learned output is a trajectory, a conventional tracker may consume it; direct actuation needs explicit vehicle interface and fallback evidence."}
    _control_output_claim={"claim":"Control consumes planned trajectory/target speed, odometry/state and steering/chassis status; outputs may be lateral steering/steering-rate, longitudinal target speed/acceleration or combined control messages/horizons. The vehicle interface converts those to available steering/throttle/brake/target-speed commands. Tracking error, stability, comfort, feasibility, timing and diagnostics are separate evaluation outputs.","locator":"Autoware Pure Pursuit/MPC/PID/follower APIs; Apollo control guide; ISO 23792-1:2026 preview; conditional mechanical/powertrain role task records; inspected 2026-10-28.","source_ids":["D166-AUTOWARE-TRAJECTORY-FOLLOWER-CONTROL","D166-AUTOWARE-PURE-PURSUIT-CONTROLLER-CURRENT","D166-AUTOWARE-MPC-LATERAL-CONTROLLER","D166-AUTOWARE-PID-LONGITUDINAL-CONTROLLER","D166-APOLLO-CONTROL-TUNING","D166-ISO-23792-1-2026-MCS","D166-LUCID-PLANNING-CONTROL-ENGINEER-2026-10-23","D166-TESLA-VEHICLE-DYNAMICS-CONTROLS-2026-10-23","D166-ROLE-POWERTRAIN-CONTROLS-2026-10-22"],"area_context":"Planner/state-estimation to controller to vehicle-interface handoff. The indexed role examples conditionally connect control/dynamics assignments to mechanical and powertrain engineering; no role route is created or changed.","claim_limit":"API/standard examples do not define a universal message schema. Role evidence is assignment/employer-specific, and D166 has no canonical role-area routes in this index.","relationship_context":"Trajectory + localized state -> controller command -> actuator conversion -> measured response -> tracking/stability/comfort/timing and diagnostic/fallback metrics. Mechanical tasks concern dynamics/control; powertrain examples concern propulsion/braking response, not every responsibility in those roles."}
    _control_future_output_claim={"claim":"End-to-end driving research varies in whether a learned model emits intermediate plans, trajectories or direct low-level actuation. The output contract determines whether a modular tracker, vehicle interface, actuator conversion, safety monitor and closed-loop test are required.","locator":"2026 end-to-end architecture review, output-interface and controller-coupling comparison; inspected 2026-10-28.","source_ids":["D166-E2E-ADS-ARCHITECTURES-CONTROL-REVIEW-2026","D166-FOUNDATION-MODELS-ADS-SURVEY-2026","D166-AUTOWARE-TRAJECTORY-FOLLOWER-CONTROL"],"area_context":"Future/experimental integration output context, distinguishing a trajectory consumed by a tracker from direct actuation.","claim_limit":"Review taxonomy covers heterogeneous prototypes; it does not establish universal safety, maturity or production readiness.","relationship_context":"Learned output -> inspect its actual contract -> route to tracker or vehicle interface -> validate execution timing, fallback, constraints and safety. The interface description does not create dependencies between unrelated items."}
    _control_future_standard_claim={"claim":"ISO 23792-1:2026 is a published, ODD-specific L3 motorway-chauffeur framework; ISO/FDIS 19484 is a draft for L4 motorway sub-trips. Their DDT, intervention/re-engagement, MRM and test requirements contextualize future motion-control systems but do not specify a universal low-level controller.","locator":"Official ISO records and public previews for ISO 23792-1:2026 and ISO/FDIS 19484, checked 2026-10-28.","source_ids":["D166-ISO-23792-1-2026-MCS","D166-ISO-19484-FDIS-HMCS-2026"],"area_context":"Feature-level future standards context for limited-access motorway automation.","claim_limit":"The L3 standard is feature- and jurisdiction-specific; the L4 text remains draft, and full normative text is not public.","relationship_context":"Target feature/ODD -> consult applicable edition and jurisdiction -> translate DDT/intervention requirements to controller/interface tests. Draft requirements remain pending."}
